{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JTjMqJAY8zknPmtrcqqtGC","type":"tool_result","content":"906\t## The proportion that organizes everything\n907\t\n908\t> **Pods consume node resources and PVCs consume PV resources.**\n909\t\n910\tFour terms, exact parallel. A Pod does not create CPU; it requests some and the scheduler\n911\tfinds a node. A PVC does not create storage; it requests some and a control loop finds a PV.\n912\t\n913\t## Supply and demand\n914\t\n915\t| | PersistentVolume | PersistentVolumeClaim |\n916\t|---|---|---|\n917\t| Is | **supply** — *\"a piece of storage in the cluster\"* | **demand** — *\"a request for storage by a user\"* |\n918\t| Scope | **cluster-scoped** | **namespaced** |\n919\t| Created by | an administrator, or a provisioner | a user |\n920\t| Carries | the real storage's implementation details | a size, an access mode, a reclaim expectation |\n921\t\n922\t*\"Details of the storage itself are described in the PersistentVolume object.\"* A claim says\n923\t*how much* and *how*. It does not say *which array*.\n924\t\n925\t**The scoping is derived, not arbitrary.** Supply is a cluster-wide resource like a node; it\n926\tbelongs to no team. Demand belongs to a specific application in a specific namespace. And\n927\tthe namespace is load-bearing at consumption time: *\"claims must exist in the same namespace\n928\tas the Pod using the claim.\"*\n929\t\n930\t## The routing fact — the highest-value sentence in the section\n931\t\n932\t> **A Pod references the claim, never the volume.**\n933\t>\n934\t> *\"Pods use claims as volumes. The cluster inspects the claim to find the bound volume and\n935\t> mounts that volume for a Pod.\"*\n936\t\n937\tThis is what lets one manifest work on a cluster backed by EBS and a cluster backed by Ceph\n938\twithout changing a character. **Trap #64 is the reversal of it.**\n939\t\n940\t## ⚑ Attribution defect a later chapter must not inherit\n941\t\n942\tCh 11 §2 says *\"Chapter 4 taught you that a PersistentVolume is cluster-scoped while a\n943\tPersistentVolumeClaim is namespaced.\"* **`chapter-04` contains zero occurrences of\n944\t\"PersistentVolumeClaim\"** — verified. Ch 4 §3 names Nodes, PersistentVolumes and\n945\tStorageClasses as its cluster-scoped examples; the PV half is solid, the PVC half is new in\n946\tCh 11. **Practice Q4's `[retrieval: ch4]` tag is misaligned on the same root cause.** See\n947\t`retrieval-log.md`.\n948\t\n949\t## Binding\n950\t\n951\t*\"A control loop in the control plane watches for new PVCs, finds a matching PV (if\n952\tpossible), and binds them together.\"* Naming it as a control loop is free retrieval on the\n953\tbook's spine concept (Ch 3 §6) — there is no separate storage subsystem, just a controller\n954\tdoing what controllers do.\n955\t\n956\tTwo properties, both counter-intuitive **in the same direction**: readers expect binding to\n957\tbe looser than it is.\n958\t\n959\t1. **Exclusive and one-to-one.** *\"A PVC to PV binding is a one-to-one mapping.\"* A 50Gi PV\n960\t   satisfying a 20Gi claim has **no** leftover 30Gi for anyone else. **Trap #63.**\n961\t2. **An unmatched claim waits forever.** *\"Claims will remain unbound indefinitely if a\n962\t   matching volume does not exist.\"* Not an error, not a timeout, not an alertable event.\n963\t   The claim sits and the Pod stays `Pending`.\n964\t\n965\t**Capacity is one filter among several.** A claim may also constrain by `storageClassName`,\n966\tby label selector (`matchLabels` and `matchExpressions` **ANDed together**, reusing the Ch 4\n967\t§5 selector grammar), and by `volumeName` naming a specific PV. \"Big enough, so it fits\" is\n968\tthe reasonable wrong answer — Ch 11 Practice Q13 exists for it.\n969\t\n970\t## Storage Object in Use Protection\n971\t\n972\tKubernetes will not pull storage out from under a running Pod. Deleting an in-use PVC leaves\n973\tit `Terminating` with `kubernetes.io/pvc-protection` in its finalizers; *\"PVC removal is\n974\tpostponed until the PVC is no longer actively used by any Pods.\"* If you have watched\n975\t`kubectl delete pvc` hang, this is why.\n976\t\n977\t⚠ **\"finalizer\" is used here and never defined.** See `glossary.md`.\n978\t\n979\t## Constraints on later chapters\n980\t\n981\t- **Ch 13 §2** owns the *diagnosis*: from the Pod's point of view an unbindable claim is\n982\t  indistinguishable from a scheduling failure. Ch 11 supplies the mechanism.\n983\t- Any figure must terminate the Pod's line **at the claim**. A line from Pod to PV\n984\t  contradicts the section's Fixed Point.\n985\t\n986\t## See also\n987\t\n988\t[[pv-phases]] · [[storageclass-and-provisioning]] · [[access-modes]] · [[reclaim-policies]]\n989\t=== END WRITE ===\n990\t\n991\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/pv-phases.md ===\n992\t# Concept — PersistentVolume phases\n993\t\n994\t**Definition home:** Ch 11 §2 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n995\t**Status:** ⚑ **LIVE SOURCE DISAGREEMENT — do not assert a count.**\n996\t\n997\t---\n998\t\n999\t## The four the teaching documentation names\n1000\t\n1001\t| Phase | Meaning |\n1002\t|---|---|\n1003\t| `Available` | Provisioned, and no claim has taken it |\n1004\t| `Bound` | Matched to a claim, and spoken for by that claim alone |\n1005\t| `Released` | The claim is gone; the cluster has not yet finished with the volume |\n1006\t| `Failed` | Automatic reclamation was attempted and did not succeed |\n1007\t\n1008\t`[source: k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25]`\n1009\t\n1010\t## ⚑ The disagreement\n1011\t\n1012\tThe **v1 API reference** additionally documents a **`Pending`** phase, *\"used for\n1013\tPersistentVolumes that are not available\"*\n1014\t`[source: k8s-api-ref-persistentvolume-v1-2026-08-25]`. The cached depth snapshot flags the\n1015\tdisagreement against itself.\n1016\t\n1017\t**How Ch 11 handles it, and the handling is the canon:** teach the four — they describe the\n1018\tfree → bound → released arc the chapter is built on — but tell the reader that if `Pending`\n1019\tturns up as an option, **do not eliminate it on the grounds that no such phase exists.**\n1020\tIt exists. It is simply not one of the four the teaching documentation walks through.\n1021\t\n1022\t**Do not \"fix\" this by asserting a number.** The chapter's table paraphrases rather than\n1023\tquotes, per the snapshot's own retrieval note, and the ⚠ hedge is a 🪝 Snag in §2 rather than\n1024\tburied in the summary — deliberately, so the summary is not where the reader first meets\n1025\t`Pending`.\n1026\t\n1027\t**Open research gap:** a further retrieval of `/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#phase`\n1028\twould settle the count and also clear the `Released`/`Failed` prose for direct quotation.\n1029\tUntil then, paraphrase.\n1030\t\n1031\t## The examinable fact underneath the count\n1032\t\n1033\t> **`Released` is not `Available`.**\n1034\t\n1035\tA volume whose claim has been deleted has *left* the bound state without *entering* the free\n1036\tstate. Nothing binds to it. Making it reusable takes three manual steps and produces a\n1037\t**new** PV object — see [[reclaim-policies]]. **Trap #67.**\n1038\t\n1039\t`Failed` is a **storage-lifecycle** outcome, not a scheduling one. Ch 11 Bearings #2 Q3\n1040\toption D exists to reject the reader who reaches for `Failed` to describe an unschedulable\n1041\tPod's claim.\n1042\t\n1043\t## See also\n1044\t\n1045\t[[persistentvolume-and-claim]] · [[reclaim-policies]]\n1046\t=== END WRITE ===\n1047\t\n1048\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/storageclass-and-provisioning.md ===\n1049\t# Concept — StorageClass, provisioning, and `volumeBindingMode`\n1050\t\n1051\t**Definition home:** Ch 11 §3 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n1052\t**Figure:** `ch11-fig03-static-vs-dynamic-provisioning`\n1053\t**Sources:** `k8s-docs-storage-classes-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-2026-08-23`,\n1054\t`k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25`\n1055\t\n1056\t---\n1057\t\n1058\t## What a StorageClass is\n1059\t\n1060\t*\"A StorageClass provides a way for administrators to describe the classes of storage they\n1061\toffer… Kubernetes itself is unopinionated about what classes represent.\"*\n1062\t\n1063\tThat last clause does real work. Kubernetes does not know what `fast` means. It knows that\n1064\tis a name an administrator chose and attached to a provisioning configuration.\n1065\t\n1066\tThe problem statement, from the PV documentation: administrators need to *\"offer a variety\n1067\tof PersistentVolumes that differ in more ways than size and access modes, **without exposing\n1068\tusers to the details of how those volumes are implemented**.\"*\n1069\t\n1070\t**The name is the API.** *\"The name of a StorageClass object is significant, and is how users\n1071\tcan request a particular class.\"* A developer writing `storageClassName: low-latency` is\n1072\tusing that name as the entire interface to a system they know nothing else about.\n1073\t\n1074\tFields: `provisioner` (**must be specified**), `parameters` (a **passthrough** — meaningful\n1075\tto the provisioner, opaque to Kubernetes), `reclaimPolicy`.\n1076\t\n1077\t## Two ways a volume comes to exist\n1078\t\n1079\t- **Static** — *\"A cluster administrator creates a number of PVs. They carry the details of\n1080\t  the real storage.\"* Supply first, demand later.\n1081\t- **Dynamic** — *\"When none of the static PVs the administrator created match a user's\n1082\t  PersistentVolumeClaim, the cluster **may** try to dynamically provision a volume specially\n1083\t  for the PVC.\"*\n1084\t\n1085\t**That \"may\" is precise, and the two conditions are the Fixed Point:**\n1086\t\n1087\t> *\"This provisioning is based on StorageClasses: the PVC must request a storage class\n1088\t> **and** the administrator must have created and configured that class for dynamic\n1089\t> provisioning to occur.\"*\n1090\t\n1091\tOne without the other is a claim that waits forever — not an error, not a timeout, not a\n1092\tfailure event. Silence.\n1093\t\n1094\t## The default class, and the opt-out that catches people\n1095\t\n1096\t| What the PVC has | What happens |\n1097\t|---|---|\n1098\t| No `storageClassName` field at all | The default StorageClass is used, if one exists |\n1099\t| `storageClassName: \"\"` | Dynamic provisioning is **disabled for that claim** |\n1100\t\n1101\t*\"Claims that request the class `\"\"` effectively disable dynamic provisioning for\n1102\tthemselves.\"* Such a claim binds **only** to a PV whose class is also empty. **Trap #69**, and\n1103\tit is an opt-out, not a shrug.\n1104\t\n1105\tA cluster may have **no** default at all, in which case a classless PVC is not rejected — it\n1106\t*\"creates as you defined it, and the `storageClassName` of that PVC remains unset until a\n1107\tdefault becomes available.\"* If a default appears later, the control plane retroactively\n1108\tupdates the waiting PVCs. A control loop doing exactly what control loops do.\n1109\t\n1110\t⚠ **Do not turn the default class into a precondition for provisioning.** A claim naming\n1111\t`fast` provisions against `fast` whether or not any class is marked default. Ch 11 Practice\n1112\tQ6 option D exists to reject that conflation.\n1113\t\n1114\t## `volumeBindingMode` — the scheduling join\n1115\t\n1116\t*\"Controls when volume binding and dynamic provisioning should occur. When unset,\n1117\t`Immediate` mode is used by default.\"*\n1118\t\n1119\t`Immediate` binds as soon as the PVC exists, which for topology-constrained storage\n1120\t*\"may result in unschedulable Pods.\"* `WaitForFirstConsumer` *\"delays the binding and\n1121\tprovisioning of a PersistentVolume until a Pod using the PersistentVolumeClaim is created,\"*\n1122\tafter which volumes are *\"selected or provisioned conforming to the topology that is\n1123\tspecified by the Pod's scheduling constraints. These include, but are not limited to,\n1124\tresource requirements, node selectors, pod affinity and anti-affinity, and taints and\n1125\ttolerations.\"*\n1126\t\n1127\t**That list is Ch 7 §2's filter phase item for item.** Scheduling and storage placement are\n1128\tnot two decisions that interact; they are one decision, and making them separately produces\n1129\ta Pod that cannot run.\n1130\t\n1131\t**Depth note, and preserve it.** Ch 11 marks this 🔭 Closer Look — above what this book's\n1132\treading of the KCNA Storage competency requires. What a later chapter should carry forward is\n1133\t**the consequence, not the field name**: *volume binding can wait on scheduling, because\n1134\tbinding a volume before the scheduler has picked a node can bind it somewhere the Pod cannot\n1135\tgo.* A reader who remembers that sentence and forgets `volumeBindingMode` has taken the right\n1136\tthing.\n1137\t\n1138\tTwo operational notes: support is per-plugin (*\"CSI volumes, provided that the specific CSI\n1139\tdriver supports this\"*), and it breaks if you bypass the scheduler — *\"if `nodeName` is used\n1140\tin this case, the scheduler will be bypassed and PVC will remain in `pending` state.\"*\n1141\t\n1142\t## The absent-component instance\n1143\t\n1144\tA StorageClass is a **description of a provisioning capability**, not the capability. Name a\n1145\tprovisioner nobody deployed and you get a valid API object that provisions nothing. See\n1146\t[[absent-component-pattern]] — ⚑ **the instance numbering is contested; read that shard\n1147\tbefore drafting Ch 13 §7.**\n1148\t\n1149\t## Deliberately out of scope\n1150\t\n1151\t`allowVolumeExpansion` and `mountOptions` appear in the shipped example manifest and get no\n1152\ttreatment. That is a decision, not an oversight — the example is a real StorageClass rather\n1153\tthan a trimmed one, and nothing later depends on those fields. **Do not gloss\n1154\t`allowVolumeExpansion` without a source**; the sentence a prior audit proposed\n1155\t(*\"you can only use the volume expansion feature to grow a Volume, not to shrink it\"*) is not\n1156\tattested in any cached snapshot.\n1157\t\n1158\t## See also\n1159\t\n1160\t[[persistentvolume-and-claim]] · [[reclaim-policies]] · [[csi]]\n1161\t=== END WRITE ===\n1162\t\n1163\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/access-modes.md ===\n1164\t# Concept — Access modes\n1165\t\n1166\t**Definition home:** Ch 11 §4 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n1167\t**Figure:** `ch11-fig04-access-modes-and-reclaim-policies` (left panel)\n1168\t**Source:** `k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25`\n1169\t**Exam weight:** carries **trap #65**, which Ch 11 identifies as the chapter's single\n1170\thighest-yield fact.\n1171\t\n1172\t---\n1173\t\n1174\t## The four\n1175\t\n1176\t| Mode | CLI | Unit | Meaning |\n1177\t|---|---|---|---|\n1178\t| `ReadWriteOnce` | RWO | **node** | mounted read-write by a single node |\n1179\t| `ReadOnlyMany` | ROX | **nodes** | mounted read-only by many nodes |\n1180\t| `ReadWriteMany` | RWX | **nodes** | mounted read-write by many nodes |\n1181\t| `ReadWriteOncePod` | RWOP | **Pod** | mounted read-write by a single Pod |\n1182\t\n1183\t## The Fixed Point\n1184\t\n1185\t> **RWO counts nodes. RWOP is the one that counts Pods.**\n1186\t>\n1187\t> *\"ReadWriteOnce access mode still can allow multiple Pods to access the volume when the\n1188\t> Pods are running on the same node. For single Pod access, use ReadWriteOncePod.\"*\n1189\t\n1190\t**The existence of a fourth mode devoted entirely to the Pod/node distinction is itself the\n1191\tevidence that the distinction is hard to hold.** Kubernetes shipped a whole access mode\n1192\tbecause people kept assuming RWO already meant it. That argument is the most durable form of\n1193\tthe fact and should survive any restatement.\n1194\t\n1195\tShipped mnemonic: **read the last word as the unit.** Three modes leave the unit implicit\n1196\tand one spells it out; the one that spells it out is the one that differs.\n1197\t\n1198\t## Why the unit is the node\n1199\t\n1200\tNot a Kubernetes fact, and **Ch 11 labels it as such**: two independent filesystem drivers,\n1201\teach caching metadata for the same block device, will overwrite each other's bookkeeping.\n1202\tOrdinary storage knowledge the platform inherits from the hardware. A clustered filesystem\n1203\texists to coordinate exactly that.\n1204\t\n1205\t⚠ **Preserve the provenance split.** *\"That is not a Kubernetes fact, and no Kubernetes\n1206\tdocument will tell you so.\"* Do not promote it to a sourced claim downstream.\n1207\t\n1208\t## Two facts that are easy to state and easy to forget\n1209\t\n1210\t1. **One mode at a time.** *\"A volume can only be mounted using one access mode at a time,\n1211\t   even if it supports many. For example, a NFS volume can be mounted as ReadWriteOnce on one\n1212\t   node and read-only on another node at the same time, but not on the same node for both\n1213\t   read-write and read-only.\"* Ch 11 Practice Q11 is the documentation's exact counterexample.\n1214\t2. **It is not a permission system.** *\"A volume access mode does not enforce write\n1215\t   protection. For example, if you provision a ReadOnlyMany volume, it does not prevent an\n1216\t   application from writing to the mounted volume if the Pod's securityContext allows write\n1217\t   access.\"* ⚠ Forward pointer to **Ch 12 §5**.\n1218\t\n1219\t## Capability, not policy\n1220\t\n1221\t*\"A PersistentVolume can have any access mode supported by the resource provider. For\n1222\texample, NFS can support multiple read/write clients, but an iSCSI volume can only be used by\n1223\tone client at a time.\"* An access mode is something the storage **has**, not something you\n1224\tchoose for it.\n1225\t\n1226\t## See also\n1227\t\n1228\t[[reclaim-policies]] (the other half of the same question) · [[persistentvolume-and-claim]]\n1229\t=== END WRITE ===\n1230\t\n1231\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/reclaim-policies.md ===\n1232\t# Concept — Reclaim policies, and where the decision was made\n1233\t\n1234\t**Definition home:** Ch 11 §4 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n1235\t**Figure:** `ch11-fig04-access-modes-and-reclaim-policies` (right panel)\n1236\t**Exam weight:** carries **traps #66, #67, #68**.\n1237\t**Sources:** `k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-2026-08-23`,\n1238\t`k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-storage-classes-2026-08-25`,\n1239\t`k8s-api-ref-persistentvolume-v1-2026-08-25`\n1240\t\n1241\t---\n1242\t\n1243\t## The three\n1244\t\n1245\t| Policy | PV object | Backing asset | Data |\n1246\t|---|---|---|---|\n1247\t| `Retain` | kept | kept | kept |\n1248\t| `Delete` | destroyed | destroyed | destroyed |\n1249\t| `Recycle` | **deprecated** | — | — |\n1250\t\n1251\t*\"The reclaim policy for a PersistentVolume tells the cluster what to do with the volume\n1252\tafter it has been released of its claim.\"*\n1253\t\n1254\t## `Retain` means kept and **stuck**, not kept and reusable\n1255\t\n1256\t*\"When the PersistentVolumeClaim is deleted, the PersistentVolume still exists and the volume\n1257\tis considered 'released'. But it is not yet available for another claim because the previous\n1258\tclaimant's data remains on the volume.\"*\n1259\t\n1260\tThe documented manual sequence:\n1261\t\n1262\t1. Delete the PersistentVolume. The storage asset still exists.\n1263\t2. Manually clean up the data on the associated storage asset.\n1264\t3. Manually delete the associated storage asset.\n1265\t\n1266\tAnd: *\"If you want to reuse the same storage asset, create a **new** PersistentVolume with\n1267\tthe same storage asset definition.\"* The released one never returns to `Available` on its\n1268\town. **Trap #67.**\n1269\t\n1270\t## `Recycle`\n1271\t\n1272\tDeprecated. *\"The recommended approach is to use dynamic provisioning.\"* Where still\n1273\tsupported it *\"performs a basic scrub (`rm -rf /thevolume/*`).\"* On an exam it is a name to\n1274\trecognize and correctly identify as retired. **Trap #68.**\n1275\t\n1276\t## ★ The inherited default — the chapter's answer to its own opening question\n1277\t\n1278\t> *\"Volumes that were dynamically provisioned inherit the reclaim policy of their\n1279\t> StorageClass, which defaults to `Delete`.\"*\n1280\t>\n1281\t> And from the class's side: *\"If no `reclaimPolicy` is specified when a StorageClass object\n1282\t> is created, it will default to `Delete`.\"*\n1283\t\n1284\tFollow the chain: no `storageClassName` → default class applies → that class set no\n1285\t`reclaimPolicy` → `Delete` → the volume inherits it → deleting the PVC destroys the disk.\n1286\t\n1287\t**Nobody in that story made a decision about the data's survival.** It was settled once, in a\n1288\tStorageClass manifest, by an administrator configuring a cluster who had never heard of the\n1289\tapplication. The documentation puts it mildly: *\"the administrator should configure the\n1290\tStorageClass according to users' expectations.\"*\n1291\t\n1292\t**Trap #66**, and the safe assumption is the opposite of the intuitive one.\n1293\t\n1294\t## The two defaults are opposite, and that is correct\n1295\t\n1296\t- **Manually created PVs default to `Retain`**\n1297\t  `[source: k8s-api-ref-persistentvolume-v1-2026-08-25]`\n1298\t- **Dynamically provisioned volumes inherit the class's, defaulting to `Delete`**\n1299\t\n1300\tNot an inconsistency. If an administrator hand-built a PV pointing at a real NFS export,\n1301\tdeleting the API object should not scrub the export. If a provisioner created a volume on\n1302\tdemand for one claim, cleaning it up when the claim is gone is the point of creating it on\n1303\tdemand. **The defaults differ because the situations differ** — keep that reasoning attached\n1304\twhenever the two defaults are stated together, or the pair reads as an API inconsistency.\n1305\t\n1306\t## The through-line worth carrying\n1307\t\n1308\tEvery one of traps #66–#68 is **a wrong guess about a default someone else set**. That is\n1309\twhat makes them dangerous rather than merely wrong: you cannot catch them by reading your own\n1310\tmanifest, because they were settled in somebody else's.\n1311\t\n1312\tOperational line the chapter gives the reader: `kubectl get storageclass` takes two seconds.\n1313\t\n1314\t## See also\n1315\t\n1316\t[[access-modes]] · [[storageclass-and-provisioning]] · [[pv-phases]] · [[statefulset-storage]]\n1317\t=== END WRITE ===\n1318\t\n1319\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/csi.md ===\n1320\t# Concept — CSI (Container Storage Interface)\n1321\t\n1322\t**Definition home:** Ch 11 §5 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage — **closes research gap G11**\n1323\t**Collected at Ch 17 §4** — see [[pluggable-interfaces]] before drafting that section.\n1324\t**Sources:** `k8s-docs-volumes-csi-and-subpath-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23`,\n1325\t`k8s-glossary-storage-terms-2026-08-25`, `csi-spec-objective-2026-08-25`,\n1326\t`kubernetes-csi-docs-deployment-2026-08-25`, `k8s-api-ref-csidriver-v1-2026-08-25`\n1327\t\n1328\t---\n1329\t\n1330\t## Definition\n1331\t\n1332\t*\"The Container Storage Interface (CSI) defines a standard interface for container\n1333\torchestration systems (like Kubernetes) to expose arbitrary storage systems to their\n1334\tcontainer workloads.\"*\n1335\t\n1336\tConsequence: *\"CSI allows vendors to create custom storage plugins for Kubernetes without\n1337\tadding them to the Kubernetes repository (out-of-tree plugins)\"* — vendors can introduce new\n1338\tstorage systems *\"without ever having to edit the core Kubernetes code.\"*\n1339\t\n1340\t## ★ The framing that matters more than the definition\n1341\t\n1342\t> CSI exists to *\"define an industry standard 'Container Storage Interface' (CSI) that will\n1343\t> enable storage vendors (SP) to develop a plugin once and have it work **across a number of\n1344\t> container orchestration (CO) systems**\"* `[source: csi-spec-objective-2026-08-25]`.\n1345\t\n1346\t**CSI is not a Kubernetes feature that vendors happen to use. It is a cross-orchestrator\n1347\tstandard that Kubernetes happens to implement** — the same shape as OCI at the image and\n1348\truntime boundary. Sourcing this to the *specification* rather than to Kubernetes\n1349\tdocumentation is deliberate and should be preserved: the point is that the contract's other\n1350\tparty is not Kubernetes.\n1351\t\n1352\t## Why it exists\n1353\t\n1354\t*\"Previously, all volume plugins were 'in-tree'. The 'in-tree' plugins were built, linked,\n1355\tcompiled, and shipped with the core Kubernetes binaries. This meant that adding a new storage\n1356\tsystem to Kubernetes (a volume plugin) required checking code into the core Kubernetes code\n1357\trepository.\"*\n1358\t\n1359\t⚠ **Provenance split, and Ch 11 labels it in reader-facing text.** The documentation records\n1360\tthe arrangement; it does **not** record what it cost the people inside it. The consequences\n1361\tCh 11 draws — vendors shipping on Kubernetes' release schedule, maintainers carrying code for\n1362\thardware they could not test — are **the book's reading**, explicitly flagged as such. Do not\n1363\tpromote them to sourced claims.\n1364\t\n1365\tFlexVolume was the earlier attempt and is superseded: *\"using an out-of-tree CSI driver is\n1366\tthe recommended way to integrate external storage with Kubernetes.\"*\n1367\t\n1368\t## What a driver is, concretely\n1369\t\n1370\t*\"A CSI driver is typically deployed in Kubernetes as two components: a controller component\n1371\tand a per-node component.\"* The controller *\"can be deployed as a Deployment or StatefulSet\n1372\ton any node in the cluster\"*; the node component *\"should be deployed on every node in the\n1373\tcluster through a DaemonSet.\"*\n1374\t\n1375\t**A CSI driver is not exotic infrastructure. It is a Deployment and a DaemonSet running\n1376\tsoftware somebody else wrote.** The shape is recognizable without further explanation:\n1377\tcluster-wide operations in one controller, node-local mounts in one agent per node.\n1378\t\n1379\tInstalling one also creates a `CSIDriver` object, which *\"captures information about a\n1380\tContainer Storage Interface (CSI) volume driver deployed on the cluster.\"*\n1381\t\n1382\t⚠ **Coverage gap:** the two-component driver architecture — promised by name at\n1383\t`chapter-02:600` — is **taught in §5 and tested in no graded item**. The checkpoint slot it\n1384\twould have used was claimed by a `volumeClaimTemplates` item during the curriculum rebalance.\n1385\tLogged in `retrieval-log.md`; the Practice set is the right home if a slot is found.\n1386\t\n1387\t## The ordering requirement\n1388\t\n1389\t*\"To use a CSI driver from a storage provider, you must **first** deploy it to your cluster.\n1390\tYou will then be able to create a Storage Class that uses that CSI driver.\"*\n1391\t\n1392\tAnd bluntly: *\"**The core of Kubernetes does not install that software for you.**\"*\n1393\t\n1394\tSee [[absent-component-pattern]] — ⚑ instance numbering contested.\n1395\t\n1396\t## CSI is an interface, not a product\n1397\t\n1398\tThere is no \"CSI storage\" to buy, any more than there is \"CNI networking.\" What you deploy is\n1399\ta **driver**, written by whoever owns the storage it speaks to. A question treating CSI as a\n1400\tstorage system rather than as the interface storage systems implement is testing exactly this\n1401\tconfusion.\n1402\t\n1403\t**Named driver examples were deliberately removed** from the shipped chapter — no cached\n1404\tsnapshot enumerates driver implementations. Open research gap: the `kubernetes-csi` drivers\n1405\tlist. Do not reintroduce named drivers without it.\n1406\t\n1407\t## CSI migration (above KCNA depth — 🔭)\n1408\t\n1409\t*\"The `CSIMigration` feature directs operations against existing in-tree plugins to\n1410\tcorresponding CSI plugins.\"* The compatibility promise is unusually strong: existing PVs\n1411\t*\"can still be used in the future without any configuration changes… even after you upgrade\n1412\tto a version of Kubernetes that doesn't have compiled-in support for that kind of storage.\"*\n1413\tManifests keep working; the machinery underneath was replaced.\n1414\t\n1415\tOperations CSI covers: *\"provisioning/delete, attach/detach, mount/unmount, and resizing of\n1416\tvolumes\"* — the storage lifecycle, mapping cleanly onto §2–§4.\n1417\t=== END WRITE ===\n1418\t\n1419\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/statefulset-storage.md ===\n1420\t# Concept — StatefulSet storage: `volumeClaimTemplates` and per-Pod claims\n1421\t\n1422\t**Definition home:** Ch 11 §6 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n1423\t**Closes the book's one deliberate forward reference** (Ch 6 §6). Reciprocal pair — **neither\n1424\thalf redefines the other.**\n1425\t**Figure:** `ch11-fig05-statefulset-pvc-pairing`\n1426\t**Sources:** `k8s-docs-statefulset-storage-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24`\n1427\t\n1428\t---\n1429\t\n1430\t## Ch 6's five deferred verbs, all closed\n1431\t\n1432\tCh 6 §6 said outright it was deferring how a StatefulSet's storage is *provisioned,\n1433\trequested, sized, reclaimed, or shared*, and that the deferral was deliberate.\n1434\t\n1435\t| Verb | Closed at |\n1436\t|---|---|\n1437\t| provisioned | Ch 11 §3 — statically, or dynamically by a provisioner named in a StorageClass |\n1438\t| requested | Ch 11 §2 — by a PersistentVolumeClaim |\n1439\t| sized | Ch 11 §2 — the claim requests capacity; binding matches it |\n1440\t| reclaimed | Ch 11 §4 — by the reclaim policy, inherited from the class for dynamic volumes |\n1441\t| shared | Ch 11 §4 — by the access mode |\n1442\t\n1443\t**What Ch 6 kept and still owns is identity.** Ch 11 §6 joins the two halves; it does not\n1444\trestate Ch 6's material.\n1445\t\n1446\t## The rule\n1447\t\n1448\t> **For each VolumeClaimTemplate entry defined in a StatefulSet, each Pod receives one\n1449\t> PersistentVolumeClaim.**\n1450\t\n1451\t**One claim per Pod *per template*** — not one claim per Pod, and not one claim for the set.\n1452\tThree replicas against two templates is six claims. Names are assembled\n1453\t**`<template>-<statefulset>-<ordinal>`**, template first.\n1454\t\n1455\tCh 11 Bearings #3 Q5 tests exactly this: option A drops the per-template clause, and option D\n1456\tgets the count right, the components right, and the **order** wrong — `cache-data-0` looks\n1457\tentirely plausible and matches nothing.\n1458\t\n1459\tStorage comes from §3's two paths, with nothing special added: *\"the storage for a given Pod\n1460\tmust either be provisioned by a PersistentVolume Provisioner based on the requested storage\n1461\tclass, or pre-provisioned by an admin.\"*\n1462\t\n1463\t## Why the claim survives a reschedule — the under-weighted half\n1464\t\n1465\tA StatefulSet's Pod keeps the same PVC for its whole lifecycle, and when rescheduled its"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59ded7e7-b8d8-4c47-ba1c-a2421bfbccc8","uuid":"708bd266-fb15-49a9-b604-59179d23bd63","timestamp":"2026-08-31T06:14:28.173Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-11/kb-manifest.md","content":"## The proportion that organizes everything\n\n> **Pods consume node resources and PVCs consume PV resources.**\n\nFour terms, exact parallel. A Pod does not create CPU; it requests some and the scheduler\nfinds a node. A PVC does not create storage; it requests some and a control loop finds a PV.\n\n## Supply and demand\n\n| | PersistentVolume | PersistentVolumeClaim |\n|---|---|---|\n| Is | **supply** — *\"a piece of storage in the cluster\"* | **demand** — *\"a request for storage by a user\"* |\n| Scope | **cluster-scoped** | **namespaced** |\n| Created by | an administrator, or a provisioner | a user |\n| Carries | the real storage's implementation details | a size, an access mode, a reclaim expectation |\n\n*\"Details of the storage itself are described in the PersistentVolume object.\"* A claim says\n*how much* and *how*. It does not say *which array*.\n\n**The scoping is derived, not arbitrary.** Supply is a cluster-wide resource like a node; it\nbelongs to no team. Demand belongs to a specific application in a specific namespace. And\nthe namespace is load-bearing at consumption time: *\"claims must exist in the same namespace\nas the Pod using the claim.\"*\n\n## The routing fact — the highest-value sentence in the section\n\n> **A Pod references the claim, never the volume.**\n>\n> *\"Pods use claims as volumes. The cluster inspects the claim to find the bound volume and\n> mounts that volume for a Pod.\"*\n\nThis is what lets one manifest work on a cluster backed by EBS and a cluster backed by Ceph\nwithout changing a character. **Trap #64 is the reversal of it.**\n\n## ⚑ Attribution defect a later chapter must not inherit\n\nCh 11 §2 says *\"Chapter 4 taught you that a PersistentVolume is cluster-scoped while a\nPersistentVolumeClaim is namespaced.\"* **`chapter-04` contains zero occurrences of\n\"PersistentVolumeClaim\"** — verified. Ch 4 §3 names Nodes, PersistentVolumes and\nStorageClasses as its cluster-scoped examples; the PV half is solid, the PVC half is new in\nCh 11. **Practice Q4's `[retrieval: ch4]` tag is misaligned on the same root cause.** See\n`retrieval-log.md`.\n\n## Binding\n\n*\"A control loop in the control plane watches for new PVCs, finds a matching PV (if\npossible), and binds them together.\"* Naming it as a control loop is free retrieval on the\nbook's spine concept (Ch 3 §6) — there is no separate storage subsystem, just a controller\ndoing what controllers do.\n\nTwo properties, both counter-intuitive **in the same direction**: readers expect binding to\nbe looser than it is.\n\n1. **Exclusive and one-to-one.** *\"A PVC to PV binding is a one-to-one mapping.\"* A 50Gi PV\n   satisfying a 20Gi claim has **no** leftover 30Gi for anyone else. **Trap #63.**\n2. **An unmatched claim waits forever.** *\"Claims will remain unbound indefinitely if a\n   matching volume does not exist.\"* Not an error, not a timeout, not an alertable event.\n   The claim sits and the Pod stays `Pending`.\n\n**Capacity is one filter among several.** A claim may also constrain by `storageClassName`,\nby label selector (`matchLabels` and `matchExpressions` **ANDed together**, reusing the Ch 4\n§5 selector grammar), and by `volumeName` naming a specific PV. \"Big enough, so it fits\" is\nthe reasonable wrong answer — Ch 11 Practice Q13 exists for it.\n\n## Storage Object in Use Protection\n\nKubernetes will not pull storage out from under a running Pod. Deleting an in-use PVC leaves\nit `Terminating` with `kubernetes.io/pvc-protection` in its finalizers; *\"PVC removal is\npostponed until the PVC is no longer actively used by any Pods.\"* If you have watched\n`kubectl delete pvc` hang, this is why.\n\n⚠ **\"finalizer\" is used here and never defined.** See `glossary.md`.\n\n## Constraints on later chapters\n\n- **Ch 13 §2** owns the *diagnosis*: from the Pod's point of view an unbindable claim is\n  indistinguishable from a scheduling failure. Ch 11 supplies the mechanism.\n- Any figure must terminate the Pod's line **at the claim**. A line from Pod to PV\n  contradicts the section's Fixed Point.\n\n## See also\n\n[[pv-phases]] · [[storageclass-and-provisioning]] · [[access-modes]] · [[reclaim-policies]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/pv-phases.md ===\n# Concept — PersistentVolume phases\n\n**Definition home:** Ch 11 §2 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n**Status:** ⚑ **LIVE SOURCE DISAGREEMENT — do not assert a count.**\n\n---\n\n## The four the teaching documentation names\n\n| Phase | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `Available` | Provisioned, and no claim has taken it |\n| `Bound` | Matched to a claim, and spoken for by that claim alone |\n| `Released` | The claim is gone; the cluster has not yet finished with the volume |\n| `Failed` | Automatic reclamation was attempted and did not succeed |\n\n`[source: k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25]`\n\n## ⚑ The disagreement\n\nThe **v1 API reference** additionally documents a **`Pending`** phase, *\"used for\nPersistentVolumes that are not available\"*\n`[source: k8s-api-ref-persistentvolume-v1-2026-08-25]`. The cached depth snapshot flags the\ndisagreement against itself.\n\n**How Ch 11 handles it, and the handling is the canon:** teach the four — they describe the\nfree → bound → released arc the chapter is built on — but tell the reader that if `Pending`\nturns up as an option, **do not eliminate it on the grounds that no such phase exists.**\nIt exists. It is simply not one of the four the teaching documentation walks through.\n\n**Do not \"fix\" this by asserting a number.** The chapter's table paraphrases rather than\nquotes, per the snapshot's own retrieval note, and the ⚠ hedge is a 🪝 Snag in §2 rather than\nburied in the summary — deliberately, so the summary is not where the reader first meets\n`Pending`.\n\n**Open research gap:** a further retrieval of `/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#phase`\nwould settle the count and also clear the `Released`/`Failed` prose for direct quotation.\nUntil then, paraphrase.\n\n## The examinable fact underneath the count\n\n> **`Released` is not `Available`.**\n\nA volume whose claim has been deleted has *left* the bound state without *entering* the free\nstate. Nothing binds to it. Making it reusable takes three manual steps and produces a\n**new** PV object — see [[reclaim-policies]]. **Trap #67.**\n\n`Failed` is a **storage-lifecycle** outcome, not a scheduling one. Ch 11 Bearings #2 Q3\noption D exists to reject the reader who reaches for `Failed` to describe an unschedulable\nPod's claim.\n\n## See also\n\n[[persistentvolume-and-claim]] · [[reclaim-policies]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/storageclass-and-provisioning.md ===\n# Concept — StorageClass, provisioning, and `volumeBindingMode`\n\n**Definition home:** Ch 11 §3 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n**Figure:** `ch11-fig03-static-vs-dynamic-provisioning`\n**Sources:** `k8s-docs-storage-classes-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-2026-08-23`,\n`k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25`\n\n---\n\n## What a StorageClass is\n\n*\"A StorageClass provides a way for administrators to describe the classes of storage they\noffer… Kubernetes itself is unopinionated about what classes represent.\"*\n\nThat last clause does real work. Kubernetes does not know what `fast` means. It knows that\nis a name an administrator chose and attached to a provisioning configuration.\n\nThe problem statement, from the PV documentation: administrators need to *\"offer a variety\nof PersistentVolumes that differ in more ways than size and access modes, **without exposing\nusers to the details of how those volumes are implemented**.\"*\n\n**The name is the API.** *\"The name of a StorageClass object is significant, and is how users\ncan request a particular class.\"* A developer writing `storageClassName: low-latency` is\nusing that name as the entire interface to a system they know nothing else about.\n\nFields: `provisioner` (**must be specified**), `parameters` (a **passthrough** — meaningful\nto the provisioner, opaque to Kubernetes), `reclaimPolicy`.\n\n## Two ways a volume comes to exist\n\n- **Static** — *\"A cluster administrator creates a number of PVs. They carry the details of\n  the real storage.\"* Supply first, demand later.\n- **Dynamic** — *\"When none of the static PVs the administrator created match a user's\n  PersistentVolumeClaim, the cluster **may** try to dynamically provision a volume specially\n  for the PVC.\"*\n\n**That \"may\" is precise, and the two conditions are the Fixed Point:**\n\n> *\"This provisioning is based on StorageClasses: the PVC must request a storage class\n> **and** the administrator must have created and configured that class for dynamic\n> provisioning to occur.\"*\n\nOne without the other is a claim that waits forever — not an error, not a timeout, not a\nfailure event. Silence.\n\n## The default class, and the opt-out that catches people\n\n| What the PVC has | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| No `storageClassName` field at all | The default StorageClass is used, if one exists |\n| `storageClassName: \"\"` | Dynamic provisioning is **disabled for that claim** |\n\n*\"Claims that request the class `\"\"` effectively disable dynamic provisioning for\nthemselves.\"* Such a claim binds **only** to a PV whose class is also empty. **Trap #69**, and\nit is an opt-out, not a shrug.\n\nA cluster may have **no** default at all, in which case a classless PVC is not rejected — it\n*\"creates as you defined it, and the `storageClassName` of that PVC remains unset until a\ndefault becomes available.\"* If a default appears later, the control plane retroactively\nupdates the waiting PVCs. A control loop doing exactly what control loops do.\n\n⚠ **Do not turn the default class into a precondition for provisioning.** A claim naming\n`fast` provisions against `fast` whether or not any class is marked default. Ch 11 Practice\nQ6 option D exists to reject that conflation.\n\n## `volumeBindingMode` — the scheduling join\n\n*\"Controls when volume binding and dynamic provisioning should occur. When unset,\n`Immediate` mode is used by default.\"*\n\n`Immediate` binds as soon as the PVC exists, which for topology-constrained storage\n*\"may result in unschedulable Pods.\"* `WaitForFirstConsumer` *\"delays the binding and\nprovisioning of a PersistentVolume until a Pod using the PersistentVolumeClaim is created,\"*\nafter which volumes are *\"selected or provisioned conforming to the topology that is\nspecified by the Pod's scheduling constraints. These include, but are not limited to,\nresource requirements, node selectors, pod affinity and anti-affinity, and taints and\ntolerations.\"*\n\n**That list is Ch 7 §2's filter phase item for item.** Scheduling and storage placement are\nnot two decisions that interact; they are one decision, and making them separately produces\na Pod that cannot run.\n\n**Depth note, and preserve it.** Ch 11 marks this 🔭 Closer Look — above what this book's\nreading of the KCNA Storage competency requires. What a later chapter should carry forward is\n**the consequence, not the field name**: *volume binding can wait on scheduling, because\nbinding a volume before the scheduler has picked a node can bind it somewhere the Pod cannot\ngo.* A reader who remembers that sentence and forgets `volumeBindingMode` has taken the right\nthing.\n\nTwo operational notes: support is per-plugin (*\"CSI volumes, provided that the specific CSI\ndriver supports this\"*), and it breaks if you bypass the scheduler — *\"if `nodeName` is used\nin this case, the scheduler will be bypassed and PVC will remain in `pending` state.\"*\n\n## The absent-component instance\n\nA StorageClass is a **description of a provisioning capability**, not the capability. Name a\nprovisioner nobody deployed and you get a valid API object that provisions nothing. See\n[[absent-component-pattern]] — ⚑ **the instance numbering is contested; read that shard\nbefore drafting Ch 13 §7.**\n\n## Deliberately out of scope\n\n`allowVolumeExpansion` and `mountOptions` appear in the shipped example manifest and get no\ntreatment. That is a decision, not an oversight — the example is a real StorageClass rather\nthan a trimmed one, and nothing later depends on those fields. **Do not gloss\n`allowVolumeExpansion` without a source**; the sentence a prior audit proposed\n(*\"you can only use the volume expansion feature to grow a Volume, not to shrink it\"*) is not\nattested in any cached snapshot.\n\n## See also\n\n[[persistentvolume-and-claim]] · [[reclaim-policies]] · [[csi]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/access-modes.md ===\n# Concept — Access modes\n\n**Definition home:** Ch 11 §4 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n**Figure:** `ch11-fig04-access-modes-and-reclaim-policies` (left panel)\n**Source:** `k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25`\n**Exam weight:** carries **trap #65**, which Ch 11 identifies as the chapter's single\nhighest-yield fact.\n\n---\n\n## The four\n\n| Mode | CLI | Unit | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `ReadWriteOnce` | RWO | **node** | mounted read-write by a single node |\n| `ReadOnlyMany` | ROX | **nodes** | mounted read-only by many nodes |\n| `ReadWriteMany` | RWX | **nodes** | mounted read-write by many nodes |\n| `ReadWriteOncePod` | RWOP | **Pod** | mounted read-write by a single Pod |\n\n## The Fixed Point\n\n> **RWO counts nodes. RWOP is the one that counts Pods.**\n>\n> *\"ReadWriteOnce access mode still can allow multiple Pods to access the volume when the\n> Pods are running on the same node. For single Pod access, use ReadWriteOncePod.\"*\n\n**The existence of a fourth mode devoted entirely to the Pod/node distinction is itself the\nevidence that the distinction is hard to hold.** Kubernetes shipped a whole access mode\nbecause people kept assuming RWO already meant it. That argument is the most durable form of\nthe fact and should survive any restatement.\n\nShipped mnemonic: **read the last word as the unit.** Three modes leave the unit implicit\nand one spells it out; the one that spells it out is the one that differs.\n\n## Why the unit is the node\n\nNot a Kubernetes fact, and **Ch 11 labels it as such**: two independent filesystem drivers,\neach caching metadata for the same block device, will overwrite each other's bookkeeping.\nOrdinary storage knowledge the platform inherits from the hardware. A clustered filesystem\nexists to coordinate exactly that.\n\n⚠ **Preserve the provenance split.** *\"That is not a Kubernetes fact, and no Kubernetes\ndocument will tell you so.\"* Do not promote it to a sourced claim downstream.\n\n## Two facts that are easy to state and easy to forget\n\n1. **One mode at a time.** *\"A volume can only be mounted using one access mode at a time,\n   even if it supports many. For example, a NFS volume can be mounted as ReadWriteOnce on one\n   node and read-only on another node at the same time, but not on the same node for both\n   read-write and read-only.\"* Ch 11 Practice Q11 is the documentation's exact counterexample.\n2. **It is not a permission system.** *\"A volume access mode does not enforce write\n   protection. For example, if you provision a ReadOnlyMany volume, it does not prevent an\n   application from writing to the mounted volume if the Pod's securityContext allows write\n   access.\"* ⚠ Forward pointer to **Ch 12 §5**.\n\n## Capability, not policy\n\n*\"A PersistentVolume can have any access mode supported by the resource provider. For\nexample, NFS can support multiple read/write clients, but an iSCSI volume can only be used by\none client at a time.\"* An access mode is something the storage **has**, not something you\nchoose for it.\n\n## See also\n\n[[reclaim-policies]] (the other half of the same question) · [[persistentvolume-and-claim]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/reclaim-policies.md ===\n# Concept — Reclaim policies, and where the decision was made\n\n**Definition home:** Ch 11 §4 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n**Figure:** `ch11-fig04-access-modes-and-reclaim-policies` (right panel)\n**Exam weight:** carries **traps #66, #67, #68**.\n**Sources:** `k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-2026-08-23`,\n`k8s-docs-persistent-volumes-depth-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-storage-classes-2026-08-25`,\n`k8s-api-ref-persistentvolume-v1-2026-08-25`\n\n---\n\n## The three\n\n| Policy | PV object | Backing asset | Data |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Retain` | kept | kept | kept |\n| `Delete` | destroyed | destroyed | destroyed |\n| `Recycle` | **deprecated** | — | — |\n\n*\"The reclaim policy for a PersistentVolume tells the cluster what to do with the volume\nafter it has been released of its claim.\"*\n\n## `Retain` means kept and **stuck**, not kept and reusable\n\n*\"When the PersistentVolumeClaim is deleted, the PersistentVolume still exists and the volume\nis considered 'released'. But it is not yet available for another claim because the previous\nclaimant's data remains on the volume.\"*\n\nThe documented manual sequence:\n\n1. Delete the PersistentVolume. The storage asset still exists.\n2. Manually clean up the data on the associated storage asset.\n3. Manually delete the associated storage asset.\n\nAnd: *\"If you want to reuse the same storage asset, create a **new** PersistentVolume with\nthe same storage asset definition.\"* The released one never returns to `Available` on its\nown. **Trap #67.**\n\n## `Recycle`\n\nDeprecated. *\"The recommended approach is to use dynamic provisioning.\"* Where still\nsupported it *\"performs a basic scrub (`rm -rf /thevolume/*`).\"* On an exam it is a name to\nrecognize and correctly identify as retired. **Trap #68.**\n\n## ★ The inherited default — the chapter's answer to its own opening question\n\n> *\"Volumes that were dynamically provisioned inherit the reclaim policy of their\n> StorageClass, which defaults to `Delete`.\"*\n>\n> And from the class's side: *\"If no `reclaimPolicy` is specified when a StorageClass object\n> is created, it will default to `Delete`.\"*\n\nFollow the chain: no `storageClassName` → default class applies → that class set no\n`reclaimPolicy` → `Delete` → the volume inherits it → deleting the PVC destroys the disk.\n\n**Nobody in that story made a decision about the data's survival.** It was settled once, in a\nStorageClass manifest, by an administrator configuring a cluster who had never heard of the\napplication. The documentation puts it mildly: *\"the administrator should configure the\nStorageClass according to users' expectations.\"*\n\n**Trap #66**, and the safe assumption is the opposite of the intuitive one.\n\n## The two defaults are opposite, and that is correct\n\n- **Manually created PVs default to `Retain`**\n  `[source: k8s-api-ref-persistentvolume-v1-2026-08-25]`\n- **Dynamically provisioned volumes inherit the class's, defaulting to `Delete`**\n\nNot an inconsistency. If an administrator hand-built a PV pointing at a real NFS export,\ndeleting the API object should not scrub the export. If a provisioner created a volume on\ndemand for one claim, cleaning it up when the claim is gone is the point of creating it on\ndemand. **The defaults differ because the situations differ** — keep that reasoning attached\nwhenever the two defaults are stated together, or the pair reads as an API inconsistency.\n\n## The through-line worth carrying\n\nEvery one of traps #66–#68 is **a wrong guess about a default someone else set**. That is\nwhat makes them dangerous rather than merely wrong: you cannot catch them by reading your own\nmanifest, because they were settled in somebody else's.\n\nOperational line the chapter gives the reader: `kubectl get storageclass` takes two seconds.\n\n## See also\n\n[[access-modes]] · [[storageclass-and-provisioning]] · [[pv-phases]] · [[statefulset-storage]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/csi.md ===\n# Concept — CSI (Container Storage Interface)\n\n**Definition home:** Ch 11 §5 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage — **closes research gap G11**\n**Collected at Ch 17 §4** — see [[pluggable-interfaces]] before drafting that section.\n**Sources:** `k8s-docs-volumes-csi-and-subpath-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23`,\n`k8s-glossary-storage-terms-2026-08-25`, `csi-spec-objective-2026-08-25`,\n`kubernetes-csi-docs-deployment-2026-08-25`, `k8s-api-ref-csidriver-v1-2026-08-25`\n\n---\n\n## Definition\n\n*\"The Container Storage Interface (CSI) defines a standard interface for container\norchestration systems (like Kubernetes) to expose arbitrary storage systems to their\ncontainer workloads.\"*\n\nConsequence: *\"CSI allows vendors to create custom storage plugins for Kubernetes without\nadding them to the Kubernetes repository (out-of-tree plugins)\"* — vendors can introduce new\nstorage systems *\"without ever having to edit the core Kubernetes code.\"*\n\n## ★ The framing that matters more than the definition\n\n> CSI exists to *\"define an industry standard 'Container Storage Interface' (CSI) that will\n> enable storage vendors (SP) to develop a plugin once and have it work **across a number of\n> container orchestration (CO) systems**\"* `[source: csi-spec-objective-2026-08-25]`.\n\n**CSI is not a Kubernetes feature that vendors happen to use. It is a cross-orchestrator\nstandard that Kubernetes happens to implement** — the same shape as OCI at the image and\nruntime boundary. Sourcing this to the *specification* rather than to Kubernetes\ndocumentation is deliberate and should be preserved: the point is that the contract's other\nparty is not Kubernetes.\n\n## Why it exists\n\n*\"Previously, all volume plugins were 'in-tree'. The 'in-tree' plugins were built, linked,\ncompiled, and shipped with the core Kubernetes binaries. This meant that adding a new storage\nsystem to Kubernetes (a volume plugin) required checking code into the core Kubernetes code\nrepository.\"*\n\n⚠ **Provenance split, and Ch 11 labels it in reader-facing text.** The documentation records\nthe arrangement; it does **not** record what it cost the people inside it. The consequences\nCh 11 draws — vendors shipping on Kubernetes' release schedule, maintainers carrying code for\nhardware they could not test — are **the book's reading**, explicitly flagged as such. Do not\npromote them to sourced claims.\n\nFlexVolume was the earlier attempt and is superseded: *\"using an out-of-tree CSI driver is\nthe recommended way to integrate external storage with Kubernetes.\"*\n\n## What a driver is, concretely\n\n*\"A CSI driver is typically deployed in Kubernetes as two components: a controller component\nand a per-node component.\"* The controller *\"can be deployed as a Deployment or StatefulSet\non any node in the cluster\"*; the node component *\"should be deployed on every node in the\ncluster through a DaemonSet.\"*\n\n**A CSI driver is not exotic infrastructure. It is a Deployment and a DaemonSet running\nsoftware somebody else wrote.** The shape is recognizable without further explanation:\ncluster-wide operations in one controller, node-local mounts in one agent per node.\n\nInstalling one also creates a `CSIDriver` object, which *\"captures information about a\nContainer Storage Interface (CSI) volume driver deployed on the cluster.\"*\n\n⚠ **Coverage gap:** the two-component driver architecture — promised by name at\n`chapter-02:600` — is **taught in §5 and tested in no graded item**. The checkpoint slot it\nwould have used was claimed by a `volumeClaimTemplates` item during the curriculum rebalance.\nLogged in `retrieval-log.md`; the Practice set is the right home if a slot is found.\n\n## The ordering requirement\n\n*\"To use a CSI driver from a storage provider, you must **first** deploy it to your cluster.\nYou will then be able to create a Storage Class that uses that CSI driver.\"*\n\nAnd bluntly: *\"**The core of Kubernetes does not install that software for you.**\"*\n\nSee [[absent-component-pattern]] — ⚑ instance numbering contested.\n\n## CSI is an interface, not a product\n\nThere is no \"CSI storage\" to buy, any more than there is \"CNI networking.\" What you deploy is\na **driver**, written by whoever owns the storage it speaks to. A question treating CSI as a\nstorage system rather than as the interface storage systems implement is testing exactly this\nconfusion.\n\n**Named driver examples were deliberately removed** from the shipped chapter — no cached\nsnapshot enumerates driver implementations. Open research gap: the `kubernetes-csi` drivers\nlist. Do not reintroduce named drivers without it.\n\n## CSI migration (above KCNA depth — 🔭)\n\n*\"The `CSIMigration` feature directs operations against existing in-tree plugins to\ncorresponding CSI plugins.\"* The compatibility promise is unusually strong: existing PVs\n*\"can still be used in the future without any configuration changes… even after you upgrade\nto a version of Kubernetes that doesn't have compiled-in support for that kind of storage.\"*\nManifests keep working; the machinery underneath was replaced.\n\nOperations CSI covers: *\"provisioning/delete, attach/detach, mount/unmount, and resizing of\nvolumes\"* — the storage lifecycle, mapping cleanly onto §2–§4.\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/statefulset-storage.md ===\n# Concept — StatefulSet storage: `volumeClaimTemplates` and per-Pod claims\n\n**Definition home:** Ch 11 §6 · **Objective:** D2.4 Storage\n**Closes the book's one deliberate forward reference** (Ch 6 §6). Reciprocal pair — **neither\nhalf redefines the other.**\n**Figure:** `ch11-fig05-statefulset-pvc-pairing`\n**Sources:** `k8s-docs-statefulset-storage-2026-08-25`, `k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24`\n\n---\n\n## Ch 6's five deferred verbs, all closed\n\nCh 6 §6 said outright it was deferring how a StatefulSet's storage is *provisioned,\nrequested, sized, reclaimed, or shared*, and that the deferral was deliberate.\n\n| Verb | Closed at |\n|---|---|\n| provisioned | Ch 11 §3 — statically, or dynamically by a provisioner named in a StorageClass |\n| requested | Ch 11 §2 — by a PersistentVolumeClaim |\n| sized | Ch 11 §2 — the claim requests capacity; binding matches it |\n| reclaimed | Ch 11 §4 — by the reclaim policy, inherited from the class for dynamic volumes |\n| shared | Ch 11 §4 — by the access mode |\n\n**What Ch 6 kept and still owns is identity.** Ch 11 §6 joins the two halves; it does not\nrestate Ch 6's material.\n\n## The rule\n\n> **For each VolumeClaimTemplate entry defined in a StatefulSet, each Pod receives one\n> PersistentVolumeClaim.**\n\n**One claim per Pod *per template*** — not one claim per Pod, and not one claim for the set.\nThree replicas against two templates is six claims. Names are assembled\n**`<template>-<statefulset>-<ordinal>`**, template first.\n\nCh 11 Bearings #3 Q5 tests exactly this: option A drops the per-template clause, and option D\ngets the count right, the components right, and the **order** wrong — `cache-data-0` looks\nentirely plausible and matches nothing.\n\nStorage comes from §3's two paths, with nothing special added: *\"the storage for a given Pod\nmust either be provisioned by a PersistentVolume Provisioner based on the requested storage\nclass, or pre-provisioned by an admin.\"*\n\n## Why the claim survives a reschedule — the under-weighted half\n\nA StatefulSet's Pod keeps the same PVC for its whole lifecycle, and when rescheduled its","numLines":560,"startLine":906,"totalLines":2002}}}
