{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ec62VrfMcL6qAAQ25UqZdW","type":"tool_result","content":"1466\t`volumeMounts` mount that same claim wherever it lands.\n1467\t\n1468\t**Note what that does *not* say.** The storage does not move. Nothing is copied. **The claim\n1469\tis a namespaced API object with no node in it, and the volume behind it is cluster-scoped.**\n1470\tNeither cares which machine `web-1` is on today.\n1471\t\n1472\tFor the involuntary case the documentation is quotable directly: *\"If a Pod associated with a\n1473\tStatefulSet fails due to node failure, and the control plane creates a replacement Pod, the\n1474\tStatefulSet retains the existing PVC. The existing volume is unaffected, and the cluster will\n1475\tattach it to the node where the new Pod is about to launch.\"*\n1476\t\n1477\t**Identity is the mechanism.** A Deployment's replacement is a new Pod with a new name and no\n1478\thistory. A StatefulSet's replacement for `web-1` must be *named* `web-1`, because the name is\n1479\thow it finds `www-web-1`. Identity and storage are one mechanism seen from two sides — this\n1480\tis the payoff, and it is what Ch 6 could not say without §2–§4.\n1481\t\n1482\t⚠ **Wording provenance:** the 2026-08-25 capture flags the 08-24 wording of the reschedule\n1483\tsentences as differing load-bearingly (PersistentVolumes vs PersistentVolumeClaims). Ch 11\n1484\tstates the reschedule behavior **in the book's own words** and quotes only the verified 08-25\n1485\tnode-failure statement. Preserve that split; do not \"upgrade\" the paraphrase to a quotation.\n1486\t\n1487\t## ★ The claims outlive the workload\n1488\t\n1489\t> **A StatefulSet's PersistentVolumeClaims are not deleted when the Pod is deleted, or when\n1490\t> the StatefulSet is deleted. This must be done manually.**\n1491\t\n1492\tGoverned by `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy` — `whenDeleted` and `whenScaled`, each\n1493\ttaking `Delete` or `Retain`, **`Retain` the default for both**. *\"PVCs from the\n1494\tvolumeClaimTemplate are not affected when their Pod is deleted.\"*\n1495\t\n1496\tThe volumes behind them persist too: *\"the PersistentVolumes associated with the Pods'\n1497\tPersistentVolume Claims are not deleted when the Pods, or StatefulSet are deleted. This must\n1498\tbe done manually.\"* **Two objects, two survival facts, stated in different places** — the\n1499\tclaims are the ones found sitting in the namespace afterward.\n1500\t\n1501\tThe reason is a stated judgment call, not an oversight: *\"This is done to ensure data safety,\n1502\twhich is generally more valuable than an automatic purge of all related StatefulSet\n1503\tresources.\"* Kubernetes chose the failure mode where forgotten volumes accumulate over the\n1504\tone where a command destroys a database. Correctly — but the cleanup is yours.\n1505\t\n1506\t**Boundary:** these policies apply to **voluntary** removal only. A node dying is not a\n1507\tscale-down.\n1508\t\n1509\t**Deliberately omitted:** a sentence about a `StatefulSetAutoDeletePVC` feature gate whose\n1510\tstability stage could not be confirmed. The `Retain` default is the safe claim. Do not\n1511\trestore the gate reference without a dated snapshot.\n1512\t\n1513\t## The opposite mechanism, held next to it\n1514\t\n1515\tA **generic ephemeral volume** also creates one PVC per Pod, and *\"when the Pod is deleted,\n1516\tthe Kubernetes garbage collector deletes the PVC.\"* **Exactly opposite deletion behavior.**\n1517\tThe difference is intent: an ephemeral volume is scratch space provisioned like real storage;\n1518\ta StatefulSet's claim is real storage created by a controller. See [[volume-types-ephemeral]].\n1519\t\n1520\t## Constraints on later chapters\n1521\t\n1522\t- **Ch 16 §6** debugs this from the application side (B6 skeleton). Refers; does not redefine.\n1523\t- ⚑ **Ch 11 §6's pointer to Ch 9 is broken.** It cites `Ch 9 §6` for the headless Service and\n1524\t  per-Pod DNS names. Verified against shipped headings: **Ch 9 §5** is the headless Service,\n1525\t  **Ch 9 §6** is kube-proxy, **Ch 9 §7** is DNS names. Fix before ship; Ch 16 §6 should cite\n1526\t  §5 and §7, not §6.\n1527\t=== END WRITE ===\n1528\t\n1529\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/pluggable-interfaces.md ===\n1530\t# Concept — The four pluggable interfaces\n1531\t\n1532\t**Collected at:** **Ch 17 §4 — \"Every Place Kubernetes Lets You In.\"** *Collects; does not\n1533\tredefine.*\n1534\t**Definition homes:** CRI → Ch 2 §4 · CNI → Ch 9 §1 · **CSI → Ch 11 §5** · CRDs → Ch 6 §8\n1535\t**Status:** ⚑ **two live naming/ordinal conflicts. Read both before drafting Ch 17 §4.**\n1536\t\n1537\t---\n1538\t\n1539\t## The set\n1540\t\n1541\t**CRI, CNI, CSI, CRDs.** B7 canonical form. Ch 11 §5 states it in exactly those four tokens,\n1542\tand Ch 11 is the chapter where the set closes.\n1543\t\n1544\t## The shape they share — what Ch 17 §4 asks the reader to state without help\n1545\t\n1546\t> **Each publishes a contract that lets someone outside the Kubernetes project supply an\n1547\t> implementation without editing core Kubernetes code.**\n1548\t\n1549\tCSI states it most explicitly — vendors can introduce new storage systems *\"without ever\n1550\thaving to edit the core Kubernetes code\"* `[source: k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23]` — and the\n1551\textension-points documentation groups CSI, CNI and CRI as infrastructure extensions alongside\n1552\tCRDs as API extensions `[source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]`.\n1553\t\n1554\t⚠ **Grouping these four as *the* four pluggable interfaces is this book's framing, not a\n1555\tKubernetes ranking.** Ch 11 Practice Q12's answer key says so. Keep that disclosure.\n1556\t\n1557\tCh 11 Practice Q12 also fixes the boundaries of the claim: **A** (\"all four are DaemonSets\")\n1558\tis false of CRDs and of the CSI controller component; **B** (\"all four are cluster-scoped API\n1559\tobjects\") is false — CRI is not an API object at all; **D** (\"same release, versioned\n1560\ttogether\") is fabricated. Ch 17 §4 must not accidentally assert any of the three.\n1561\t\n1562\t## ⚑ Conflict 1 — Chapter 2 calls the fourth one \"API extensions\"\n1563\t\n1564\t- `chapter-02:598` and `chapter-02:930` (shipped): **\"CRI, CNI, CSI, and API extensions.\"**\n1565\t- B7 ledger, `chapter-10:1866`, and Ch 11 §5: **\"CRDs.\"**\n1566\t\n1567\tBoth surface forms are in shipped text. They are partially reconciled already — Ch 2's own\n1568\tinbound pointer at line 600 links its \"API extensions\" slot to `Ch 6 §8 — CRDs` — but\n1569\t**Ch 17 §4 is the section that meets both**, and reconciling them is its job. Chapter 11\n1570\tfollows the ledger and is correct; do not weaken it.\n1571\t\n1572\t## ⚑ Conflict 2 — Chapter 9 says CNI is the *second*, and it is the outlier\n1573\t\n1574\t- `chapter-02:914` (shipped): *\"This is the **first** of four times you will see this move.\n1575\t  Storage does it. Networking does it. The API itself does it.\"* → CRI, CSI, CNI, CRDs.\n1576\t- `chapter-09:1149` and `:1650` (shipped): CNI is *\"the **second** instance\"* / *\"**Second**\n1577\t  of the four pluggable interfaces.\"*\n1578\t- `chapter-10:1866` (shipped): the reader holds **three** — CRI (Ch 2), CRDs (Ch 6), CNI\n1579\t  (Ch 9).\n1580\t- Ch 11 §5: CSI is **\"the fourth\"** / **\"the last of the four.\"**\n1581\t\n1582\tBy encounter order the reader meets CRI at Ch 2 §4 and CRDs at Ch 6 §8 before CNI, making CNI\n1583\tthe **third**. Ch 2, Ch 10 and Ch 11 agree; **Ch 9 disagrees with all three.**\n1584\t**Recommendation: retrofit Ch 9 §8 prose and summary row, \"second\" → \"third.\"**\n1585\t\n1586\t## Resolved — a collision Ch 10 predicted and Ch 11 avoided\n1587\t\n1588\tThe B6 skeleton labels Ch 11 §5 *\"**Third** of the four pluggable interfaces\"*\n1589\t(`section-skeleton.md:157`). Ch 10's Stage 14 manifest flagged that this would land a\n1590\t\"third\" on a reader just told they held three. **Shipped Ch 11 ignored the skeleton and wrote\n1591\t\"the fourth\" / \"the last.\"** Correct.\n1592\t\n1593\t**Recommendation: drop the skeleton's ordinal annotations entirely.** They encode set-order,\n1594\tnot encounter-order, and encounter-order is what the reader experiences.\n1595\t\n1596\t## See also\n1597\t\n1598\t[[csi]] · [[absent-component-pattern]] — three of the four have a documented absent-component\n1599\tfailure mode, and Ch 11 §5 is where the reader is expected to stop asking *\"is the object\n1600\tthere?\"* and start asking *\"is the component there?\"*\n1601\t=== END WRITE ===\n1602\t\n1603\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/absent-component-pattern.md ===\n1604\t# Concept — The absent-component pattern\n1605\t\n1606\t**Naming home:** Ch 3 §4 (where the name is coined)\n1607\t**Full treatment:** Ch 10 §3 and Ch 10 §8 (Zenith) · **two further instances at Ch 11 §3 and §5**\n1608\t**B3 cross-cutting theme:** #3 — retrieved *by name* at Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7\n1609\t**Status:** ⚑ **TWO CONFLICTS OPEN — canonical form, and instance count.**\n1610\t**⛔ DO NOT DRAFT Ch 13 §7 OR Ch 17 §7 UNTIL THE COUNT IS RESOLVED.**\n1611\t\n1612\t---\n1613\t\n1614\t## The rule\n1615\t\n1616\t> **An object without its component does nothing.**\n1617\t\n1618\tVerbatim from shipped Ch 3 §4; reproduced verbatim in Ch 10 §3, Ch 10 §8, and Ch 11's Voyage\n1619\tAhead. **Do not paraphrase this sentence in any chapter** — it is retrieved as a quotation.\n1620\t\n1621\tCh 10 §8's compression, which later chapters should reach for:\n1622\t\n1623\t> An object is a record of intent. Intent does not act.\n1624\t\n1625\t## ⚑ CONFLICT 1 — canonical form (carried from Ch 10's Stage 14)\n1626\t\n1627\t| Form | Where it lives | Verdict |\n1628\t|---|---|---|\n1629\t| **\"the absent-component pattern\"** | Shipped Ch 3 §4, reader-facing ×4 | **Recommended headword** |\n1630\t| \"an object without its component does nothing\" | Shipped Ch 3 §4; Ch 10 ×9; Ch 11 ×3 | The **rule sentence**. Not a name. |\n1631\t| \"The object exists; nothing happens without the component\" | B7 ledger only | **Zero occurrences in shipped text.** Do not adopt. |\n1632\t| \"Nothing Happens Without a Controller\" | Ch 10 §8 heading | A section title, not a term |\n1633\t\n1634\t**Recommendation:** headword = *the absent-component pattern* (naming home Ch 3 §4); rule\n1635\tsentence as above; instance ledger owned by Ch 10 §3.\n1636\t\n1637\t## ⚑ CONFLICT 2 — the instance count. **NEW at Ch 11. This is the blocking one.**\n1638\t\n1639\t**Two incompatible counting conventions are now in shipped and finalized text.**\n1640\t\n1641\t### Convention A — Chapter 10's, and it is graded\n1642\t\n1643\t`chapter-10:634` has the reader at three by Ch 10 §3, and `chapter-10:1567` — Practice Q18's\n1644\t**correct answer** — enumerates four by Ch 10 §7:\n1645\t\n1646\t1. `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider — **Ch 9 §3**\n1647\t2. Service whose selector matches no Pods — **Ch 9 §4**\n1648\t3. Ingress with no Ingress controller — **Ch 10 §3**\n1649\t4. NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy — **Ch 10 §7** (the only\n1650\t   silent one)\n1651\t\n1652\t`chapter-10:1795` marks option B — which names only Ch 10's two instances — wrong, with this\n1653\trationale: *\"the common error here is **undercounting**, not misnaming.\"*\n1654\t\n1655\t### Convention B — Chapter 11's, which restarts at the Ingress\n1656\t\n1657\t- `draft-v2.md:587` — *\"This is the **third** sighting\"*: Ingress, NetworkPolicy, StorageClass\n1658\t- `draft-v2.md:889` — *\"three times in three sections: an Ingress without a controller, a\n1659\t  StorageClass without a provisioner, and now this. Here is **the fourth**\"*\n1660\t- `draft-v2.md:1402` — *\"the Ingress controller, the network plugin, the provisioner, and the\n1661\t  CSI driver — **four sightings**\"*\n1662\t\n1663\t**Chapter 11 drops both Chapter 9 instances** — the exact undercount Ch 10 grades as wrong.\n1664\tA reader who answered Ch 10 Q18 correctly is told six pages later that the StorageClass is\n1665\tthe third; by the count they were graded on, it is the fifth.\n1666\t\n1667\t**`draft-v2.md:889` is also internally inconsistent**: its own three-item list already\n1668\tincludes the current (CSI) case, and the sentence then announces \"the fourth.\" It silently\n1669\tdrops the NetworkPolicy instance that line 587 included two sections earlier. **The two\n1670\tenumerations inside Chapter 11 are not the same set.**\n1671\t\n1672\t### Why this blocks Ch 13 and Ch 17\n1673\t\n1674\tCh 10's Stage 14 shard reserved instance **#5 for Ch 13 §7** (`kubectl top` with no\n1675\tmetrics-server) and **#6 for Ch 17 §7** (VPA as an addon). Chapter 11 has inserted two\n1676\tinstances and renumbered from a different origin. **The conventions now diverge by exactly\n1677\ttwo**, and whichever ordinal Ch 13 uses contradicts a graded item in the other chapter.\n1678\t\n1679\t### Recommendation\n1680\t\n1681\t1. Amend Ch 11 §3/§5/Voyage Ahead to continue Convention A (StorageClass = fifth, CSI =\n1682\t   sixth). Faithful to graded canon; the ordinals get clumsy.\n1683\t2. **Preferred: stop numbering instances in prose.** Keep the rule and the enumeration, drop\n1684\t   the ordinal. Ch 10 already had to reconcile two counts in a dedicated paragraph\n1685\t   (`chapter-10:625`); Ch 11 is the third count, and the reconciliation cost now exceeds the\n1686\t   rhetorical benefit. **The list transfers; the number breaks.**\n1687\t\n1688\tEither way, `draft-v2.md:889`'s internal contradiction needs fixing regardless.\n1689\t\n1690\t**This shard deliberately does not pick a winner.** Both conventions are recorded above so\n1691\tno drafting stage can adopt one without seeing the other.\n1692\t\n1693\t## The instance ledger (Convention A ordering, origin Ch 9 §3)\n1694\t\n1695\t| # | Instance | Where | Announces itself? |\n1696\t|---|---|---|---|\n1697\t| 1 | `type: LoadBalancer` Service, no provider | Ch 9 §3 | Yes — `<pending>` |\n1698\t| 2 | Service whose selector matches no Pods | Ch 9 §4 | Yes — traffic fails |\n1699\t| 3 | Ingress with no Ingress controller | Ch 10 §3 | Yes — requests fail loudly |\n1700\t| 4 | NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement it | Ch 10 §7 | **No — silent** |\n1701\t| 5 | **StorageClass naming a provisioner nobody deployed** | **Ch 11 §3** | Yes — claim unbound, Pod `Pending` |\n1702\t| 6 | **StorageClass naming a CSI driver never installed** | **Ch 11 §5** | Yes — same signature |\n1703\t| 7 | `kubectl top` with no metrics-server | Ch 13 §7 (planned) | Yes — command errors |\n1704\t| 8 | VPA, an addon not shipped by default | Ch 17 §7 (planned) | Yes |\n1705\t\n1706\t*Rows 5–6 are Ch 11's \"third\" and \"fourth\" under Convention B. Rows 7–8 were #5 and #6 in\n1707\tCh 10's shard and have shifted.*\n1708\t\n1709\t**Instances 5 and 6 are the same failure at two depths** — a StorageClass whose provisioner\n1710\tis a CSI driver nobody deployed *is* both — which is an argument for the un-numbered\n1711\ttreatment. Ch 11 §5's sources state it as a plain ordering requirement rather than a warning:\n1712\t*\"To use a CSI driver from a storage provider, you must **first** deploy it to your cluster,\"*\n1713\tand *\"the core of Kubernetes does not install that software for you.\"*\n1714\t\n1715\t## The asymmetry — provenance, keep the split\n1716\t\n1717\tInstances 1–3 and 5–8 announce themselves. **Instance 4 does not.** The plugin dependency and\n1718\tthe \"no effect\" consequence are **documented**\n1719\t`[source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]`. The characterisation of that failure\n1720\tas *silent* is **the book's own inference**, labelled as one in Ch 10 §7. Do not promote it.\n1721\t\n1722\t## The transferable question\n1723\t\n1724\t> **What is watching this, and is it installed?**\n1725\t\n1726\tCh 10 §8 hands this to the reader; Ch 11 §5 restates it as *\"you stop asking 'is the object\n1727\tthere?' and start asking 'is the **component** there?'\"* Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7 should invoke\n1728\tit in these words.\n1729\t\n1730\t## Constraints on later chapters\n1731\t\n1732\t- Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7: retrieve **by name**; do not re-derive. **Blocked on Conflict 2.**\n1733\t- Any chapter adding an instance: extend the table, do not restate the rule.\n1734\t- **Never illustrate an instance as a broken or blocked object.** Every genuine instance is a\n1735\t  **correct** object with nothing wrong with it. Ch 10 Practice Q18 option D is built on that\n1736\t  distinction (a mismatched `pathType` is a manifest bug, not an instance), and Ch 11 §3's\n1737\t  framing depends on it too: *\"`kubectl get` shows it, `kubectl describe` shows a sensible\n1738\t  spec, and nothing happens.\"*\n1739\t=== END WRITE ===\n1740\t\n1741\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n1742\t# Objective Coverage — KCNA\n1743\t\n1744\tObjective IDs (`D1.1`, `D2.1`, …) are a **Lodestar convention** from B1 domain analysis. CNCF\n1745\tpublishes named competencies under each domain but does **not** number them and does **not**\n1746\tpublish sub-competency weights. Domain-level weights are the only level the sources state.\n1747\t\n1748\t**Domain weights (2025-11-24 four-domain blueprint):** D1 Kubernetes Fundamentals 44% ·\n1749\t**D2 Container Orchestration 28%** · D3 Cloud Native Application Delivery 16% ·\n1750\tD4 Cloud Native Architecture 12%.\n1751\t\n1752\t> **BACKFILL REQUIRED.** Stage 14 did not run for Chapters 1–9. Rows below cover Chapters 10\n1753\t> and 11, plus the D2.1 first-coverage fact verified against shipped Ch 9. Objectives D1.x,\n1754\t> D2.3, D3.x and D4.x are unlogged. Source of truth for the backfill:\n1755\t> `.pipeline-state/book-outline/chapter-lineup.md` and `domain-analysis.md`.\n1756\t\n1757\t---\n1758\t\n1759\t## Objective-level\n1760\t\n1761\t| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n1762\t|---|---|---|---|\n1763\t| D2.1 — Networking | Chapter 9 | deep — completed Chapter 10 | — |\n1764\t| D2.2 — Security | Chapter 10 (boundary only) | partial — NetworkPolicy only; full coverage lands Chapter 12 | — |\n1765\t| **D2.4 — Storage** | **Chapter 11** | **deep — sole coverage; no other chapter is assigned D2.4** | — |\n1766\t\n1767\t**D2.2 note.** B1 sequencing implication #6: NetworkPolicy sits in both D2.1 and D2.2; teach\n1768\tit once, in Networking, and cross-bear from Security. Chapter 10 is the sole definition home;\n1769\tChapter 12 §9 refers and must not duplicate.\n1770\t\n1771\t**D2.4 note.** B2 sequencing constraint: *\"Storage precedes StatefulSet's full treatment —\n1772\tCh 6 introduces, Ch 11 completes, cross-beared both ways.\"* Honored. Ch 6 §6's five deferred\n1773\tverbs (provisioned / requested / sized / reclaimed / shared) are all closed at Ch 11 §4's\n1774\treconciliation table; Ch 6 retains **identity**, which Ch 11 §6 joins rather than restates.\n1775\t\n1776\t## Concept-level — D2.4, all 20 B1 concepts\n1777\t\n1778\tWalked row by row against `domain-analysis.md:232–255`. **20 of 20 covered. No gaps.**\n1779\t\n1780\t| B1 concept | Covered in | Depth |\n1781\t|---|---|---|\n1782\t| PersistentVolume (PV) | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n1783\t| PV lifecycle independence | Ch 11 §1, §2 | deep |\n1784\t| PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n1785\t| Pods:PVCs :: node resources:Pods | Ch 11 §2 | deep — quoted, not paraphrased |\n1786\t| StorageClass | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n1787\t| Static provisioning | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n1788\t| Dynamic provisioning | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n1789\t| Disabling dynamic provisioning (`\"\"`) | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n1790\t| Binding | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n1791\t| Unbound claims | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n1792\t| Using (Pod references a PVC) | Ch 11 §2 | deep — the section's Fixed Point |\n1793\t| Reclaiming | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1794\t| Retain | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1795\t| Delete | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1796\t| Recycle | Ch 11 §4 | adequate — named, deprecation stated |\n1797\t| ReadWriteOnce (RWO) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1798\t| ReadOnlyMany (ROX) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1799\t| ReadWriteMany (RWX) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1800\t| ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n1801\t| StatefulSet + PV | Ch 11 §6 | deep |\n1802\t\n1803\t## Trap coverage — D2.4\n1804\t\n1805\t**7 of 7 (#63–#69) addressed**, verified line by line against `domain-analysis.md:571–577`.\n1806\tThe Exam Alert reproduces the complete D2 storage block in order with faithful corrections.\n1807\t\n1808\t| Trap | Where corrected |\n1809\t|---|---|\n1810\t| #63 \"A PVC binds to any PV that's big enough\" | §2 + Bearings #1 Q4 + Practice Q13 |\n1811\t| #64 Reversing PV and PVC | §2 Fixed Point + Bearings #1 Q3 + Practice Q2 |\n1812\t| #65 \"ReadWriteOnce means one Pod\" | §4 Fixed Point + Bearings #2 Q1 |\n1813\t| #66 \"Deleting a PVC always keeps the data\" | §4 + Bearings #2 Q2 + Practice Q3 |\n1814\t| #67 \"Retain means immediately reusable\" | §2, §4 + Bearings #2 Q4 + Practice Q15 |\n1815\t| #68 Expecting `Recycle` to be live | §4 |\n1816\t| #69 Class `\"\"` uses the default | §3 + Bearings #2 Q5 |\n1817\t\n1818\tThe chapter's own arithmetic about trap distribution checks out: §4's *\"five of the seven…\n1819\tfour of them are here\"* (#69 in §3, #65–#68 in §4) and Bearings #2's *\"four of the exam\n1820\ttraps… in this checkpoint alone\"* (Q1→#65, Q2→#66, Q4→#67, Q5→#69).\n1821\t\n1822\t## Research gaps closed\n1823\t\n1824\t| Gap | Description | Status |\n1825\t|---|---|---|\n1826\t| **G11** | *CNI, CSI, and CRI as the pluggable interfaces — \"CSI is entirely absent.\"* | **Closed by Ch 11 §5.** Sourced to the CSI specification's own objective statement, plus the driver's two-component deployment shape. |\n1827\t| **G12** | *Volume types other than PV/PVC: emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret volumes, projected, ephemeral.* | **Closed by Ch 11 §1**, which covers all five plus `downwardAPI`, `subPath`, `nfs`, and `local`. |\n1828\t| **G25** | Gateway API detail | Closed by Ch 10 §5. |\n1829\t\n1830\t## Research gaps still open that touch Chapter 11\n1831\t\n1832\t- **PV phase count.** The concept page enumerates four, the v1 API reference five. A further\n1833\t  retrieval of `/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#phase` would settle it and clear\n1834\t  the `Released`/`Failed` prose for direct quotation. See `concepts/pv-phases.md`.\n1835\t- **CSI driver implementations.** No cached snapshot enumerates them; the shipped chapter\n1836\t  genericized its three named drivers accordingly. Target: the `kubernetes-csi` drivers list.\n1837\t- **`allowVolumeExpansion` semantics.** The grow-not-shrink sentence is not attested in any\n1838\t  cached snapshot and was **not** written into the chapter. Do not add it without one.\n1839\t- **No exam-logistics source is cached.** Unchanged from Ch 10. Chapter 11 complies with the\n1840\t  standing constraint — its header discloses that *\"CNCF publishes four domain weights and no\n1841\t  sub-competency weights; the D2.4 identifier is this book's own numbering, not CNCF's.\"*\n1842\t\n1843\t---\n1844\t\n1845\t*Stage 14 · Chapters 10–11 · 2026-08-25.*\n1846\t=== END WRITE ===\n1847\t\n1848\t=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n1849\t# Retrieval-Practice Ledger — KCNA\n1850\t\n1851\tWhich earlier-chapter topics have been retrieval-tested, and where. Targets are set by B3\n1852\tretrieval architecture: Ch 3 at 10%, Ch 4 at 15%, then **20–25% through Ch 18**, with a\n1853\t**spacing floor from Ch 8 on — at least one item must come from ≥4 chapters back.**\n1854\t\n1855\tSoundings are **excluded from the budget** by design: skill Part 11 requires them to be\n1856\tanswerable from prerequisites, which in this book means earlier chapters, so every Soundings\n1857\tblock is already a spaced retrieval event. Counting them would distort their calibration\n1858\tpurpose.\n1859\t\n1860\t> ⚠ **`.pipeline-state/book-outline/retrieval-architecture.md` on disk contains a\n1861\t> blocked-write error message, not the B3 artifact.** The substantive design survives as\n1862\t> prose inside that file and the targets above are taken from it, but the file should be\n1863\t> regenerated.\n1864\t\n1865\t> **BACKFILL REQUIRED.** Stage 14 did not run for Chapters 1–9. Their `[retrieval: chN]` tags\n1866\t> are in the shipped files and unlogged here. Chapters 10 and 11 are complete.\n1867\t\n1868\t---\n1869\t\n1870\t## Backward — earlier material tested in a later chapter\n1871\t\n1872\t| Tested topic | Original chapter | Retested in |\n1873\t|---|---|---|\n1874\t| Service-type ladder; HTTP vs non-HTTP exposure | ch 9 §3 | ch 10 — Bearings #1 Q1; Practice Q2 |\n1875\t| The absent-component rule, stated back | ch 3 §4 | ch 10 — Bearings #1 Q5; Practice Q18 |\n1876\t| Control loop / the controller pattern | ch 3 §6 | ch 10 — Practice Q7 |\n1877\t| Labels and selectors as the universal join | ch 4 §5 | ch 10 — Bearings #3 Q1; Practice Q16 |\n1878\t| The writable container layer; what a restart discards | ch 2 §2 | **ch 11 — Bearings #1 Q2** |\n1879\t| The four pluggable interfaces, as a set | ch 2 §4/§5 | **ch 11 — Practice Q12** |\n1880\t| ServiceAccount token via a projected volume | ch 5 §6 | **ch 11 — Practice Q1** |\n1881\t| StatefulSet ordinal identity | ch 6 §6 | **ch 11 — Bearings #3 Q3** ⚠️ thin |\n1882\t| Filter phase; the unschedulable Pod | ch 7 §2 | **ch 11 — Bearings #2 Q3** |\n1883\t| The absent-component pattern | ch 10 §3 | **ch 11 — Practice Q8** |\n1884\t\n1885\t## Chapter 11 compliance\n1886\t\n1887\t| Check | Target | Actual | Verdict |\n1888\t|---|---|---|---|\n1889\t| Retrieval share of graded items | 20–25% (B3); outline target 20% | **7 of 32 = 21.9%** (15 Bearings + 17 Practice) | ✅ |\n1890\t| Spacing floor (≥4 chapters back) | ≥1 item | ch 2 is **nine** back, ×2 items | ✅ |\n1891\t| Tagged items land on covered material | 7 of 7 | **6 of 7** — see ⚑ below | ❌ |\n1892\t| Question inventory | 8 Soundings, ≥10 Bearings across ≥2 checkpoints | 8 + 15 (3 × 5) + 17 = **40** | ✅ |\n1893\t\n1894\t**Soundings note.** Ch 11's block is unusually load-bearing: **six of its eight questions are\n1895\tretrieval** from Ch 2 §2/§4, Ch 4 §3, Ch 5 §1/§6, Ch 6 §6, Ch 6 §8, Ch 9 §1 and Ch 10's\n1896\tclosing. Excluded from the budget per B3, but worth recording — the chapter gets substantially\n1897\tmore spaced retrieval than its 21.9% suggests.\n1898\t\n1899\t## ⚑ One failed retrieval anchor — Chapter 11 Practice Q4\n1900\t\n1901\tQ4 asks the reader to identify \"PersistentVolume cluster-scoped; PersistentVolumeClaim\n1902\tnamespaced\" and tags it **`[retrieval: ch4]`**.\n1903\t\n1904\t**`grep -c \"PersistentVolumeClaim\" chapter-04-records-of-intent.md` → 0.** Chapter 4 §3 names\n1905\tNodes, PersistentVolumes and StorageClasses as its cluster-scoped examples; it never mentions\n1906\tPVCs. The B7 ledger corroborates independently — it records PVC's first appearance as **Ch 6\n1907\t§6**, and ⚑6 lists only \"PersistentVolume and StorageClass\" as appearing early in Ch 4.\n1908\t\n1909\tThe **fact** is correct and Ch 11 §2 sources it properly. The **retrieval tag** is not: the\n1910\treader is asked to retrieve something never deposited. The same root cause makes Ch 11 §2's\n1911\tprose claim (*\"Chapter 4 taught you… You now have the reason rather than the rule\"*)\n1912\tover-credit Chapter 4.\n1913\t\n1914\t**Fixes, in preference order:**\n1915\t1. **Rewrite Q4 around Chapter 4's own cluster-scoped trio** (Node, PersistentVolume,\n1916\t   StorageClass) — a genuine Ch 4 anchor, already graded at `chapter-04:733` and `:1308`,\n1917\t   which holds the rate at 21.9%.\n1918\t2. Retag as untagged and let it stand as a current-chapter §2 item — **drops the rate to\n1919\t   6 of 32 = 18.8%, below band.**\n1920\t3. Leave it. Not recommended; it overstates the book's retrieval coverage.\n1921\t\n1922\t## Forward obligations Chapter 11 creates\n1923\t\n1924\t| Topic Ch 11 owns | Must be retrieved in | How |\n1925\t|---|---|---|\n1926\t| `secret` volumes on tmpfs | **Ch 12 §4** | Ch 11 hands over **half an argument by name** — file mounts vs environment variables. Ch 12 §4 owes the other half. |\n1927\t| `hostPath` as the workload-to-host hole | **Ch 12 §5** | Named and deferred explicitly; also owns the `ReadOnlyMany`-is-not-a-permission-system pointer. **Ch 12 must not re-derive the risk.** |\n1928\t| An unbound claim as a `Pending` Pod | **Ch 13 §2** | Ch 11 supplies the mechanism, Ch 13 the diagnosis. |\n1929\t| Per-replica PVC; ordinal identity + storage | **Ch 16 §6** | B6 skeleton line 243. Refers; does not redefine. ⚑ cite Ch 9 §5 and §7, **not** §6. |\n1930\t| CSI as the fourth interface | **Ch 17 §4** | *Collects; does not redefine.* ⚑ two naming conflicts — read `concepts/pluggable-interfaces.md` first. |\n1931\t| The absent-component pattern | **Ch 13 §7, Ch 17 §7** | ⛔ **BLOCKED** on the instance-count conflict. |\n1932\t\n1933\t## Open gaps\n1934\t\n1935\t**1. ⚑ NEW — the absent-component instance count is contested.** Chapter 11 restarts the\n1936\tcount at the Ingress and drops Chapter 9's two instances — the exact undercount Ch 10 Practice\n1937\tQ18 grades as wrong (*\"the common error here is undercounting\"*). Chapter 11's own two\n1938\tenumerations (`draft-v2.md:587` and `:889`) are not the same set, and `:889` calls the third\n1939\titem in its own three-item list \"the fourth.\" **Blocks Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7.** Full detail\n1940\tin `concepts/absent-component-pattern.md`.\n1941\t\n1942\t**2. CSI driver architecture is taught but never assessed.** The controller Deployment plus\n1943\tper-node DaemonSet — promised by name at `chapter-02:600` — appears in **zero of Ch 11's 40\n1944\tquestions**. The chapter's own AUTHOR-REVIEW records why: the curriculum rebalance claimed\n1945\tBearings #3's fifth slot for a `volumeClaimTemplates` item, and the two findings competed for\n1946\tone seat. **The Practice set is the right home if a slot is found** — restoring it to Bearings\n1947\t#3 would push CSI to 3 of 5 there against 1 of 17 in Practice, which is the imbalance the\n1948\trebalance was correcting.\n1949\t\n1950\t**3. north-south / east-west is taught but never assessed.** Carried from Chapter 10, where it\n1951\tappears in zero of 41 questions despite a 🪢 Mnemonic and a Chapter Summary row. **Still\n1952\topen**; Chapter 11 does not touch it.\n1953\t\n1954\t**4. ⚑ Chapter 9 undercounts the pluggable interfaces.** Ch 9 §8 calls CNI *\"the second\n1955\tinstance\"*; Ch 2 line 914, Ch 10, and Ch 11 all make it the third. **Still open.**\n1956\tRecommended retrofit: Ch 9 §8 prose and summary row, \"second\" → \"third.\"\n1957\t\n1958\t## Resolved since Chapter 10\n1959\t\n1960\t**Ordinal collision at Ch 11 §5 — RESOLVED.** Ch 10's log predicted that the B6 skeleton's\n1961\t*\"Third of the four pluggable interfaces\"* label (`section-skeleton.md:157`) would land a\n1962\t\"third\" on a reader just told they held three. **Shipped Ch 11 §5 ignored the skeleton and\n1963\twrote \"The fourth is CSI\" / \"the last of the four.\"** Consistent with Ch 2, Ch 10, and the B7\n1964\tcanonical set. **Recommendation: drop the skeleton's ordinal annotations** — they encode\n1965\tset-order, not encounter-order.\n1966\t\n1967\t---\n1968\t\n1969\t*Stage 14 · Chapters 10–11 · 2026-08-25.*\n1970\t=== END WRITE ===\n1971\t```\n1972\t\n1973\t---\n1974\t\n1975\t## Recommended fixes carried forward from this stage\n1976\t\n1977\t**Blocking — resolve before Ch 13 §7 drafts.**\n1978\t\n1979\t1. **The absent-component instance count** (⚑A). Chapter 11 undercounts against a Chapter 10 graded item that names undercounting as the error, and its own two enumerations disagree. My recommendation is to stop numbering instances in prose and keep the list.\n1980\t\n1981\t**Fix before ship — chapter-text edits, all small.**\n1982\t\n1983\t2. **§6's `Ch 9 §6` pointer** → `Ch 9 §7`, and attribute the headless Service to Ch 9 §5.\n1984\t3. **§2's Ch 4 attribution** → claim only the PV half; present the PVC half as new. Rewrite **Practice Q4** around Ch 4's own cluster-scoped trio.\n1985\t4. **Expand NFS and LUN on first use** and register NFS, LUN, iSCSI, EBS. NFS carries two graded items and is nowhere in the book in full.\n1986\t5. **`draft-v2.md:889`'s internal contradiction** — a three-item list that includes the current case, followed by \"here is the fourth.\"\n1987\t\n1988\t**Author's call — low severity.**\n1989\t\n1990\t6. §5's stale AUTHOR-REVIEW cites `ebs.csi.aws.com` \"used in this chapter's Taking Your Bearings #3\"; that string now appears only inside the comment.\n1991\t7. Soundings scoring note says six of eight questions are two-part; seven are.\n1992\t8. §1 says Ch 4 \"hedged\" the ConfigMap update rule; `chapter-04:762` states it flatly and sourced. One word.\n1993\t9. `finalizer` and `ENOSPC` need either a one-clause gloss or a B7 ambient-tier assignment.\n1994\t\n1995\t**Infrastructure.**\n1996\t\n1997\t10. **`retrieval-architecture.md` on disk is a blocked-write error message**, not the B3 artifact — unchanged since Chapter 10 flagged it. Regenerate.\n1998\t11. **The B6 skeleton's ordinal annotations should be dropped.** Chapter 11 correctly ignored one; the next chapter might not.\n1999\t\n2000\t## What I'd act on first\n2001\t\n2002\tThe instance count, ahead of the integration report's two findings. Both of those are one-line edits with obvious fixes; this one has no obvious fix, a nearer deadline, and no natural discovery point. Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7 are contractually required to retrieve the pattern by name and by count, and the two live conventions now differ by exactly two. Whichever a drafter picks, it contradicts a graded answer key in the other chapter — and nothing downstream will flag it, because both counts are internally coherent and both cite real instances."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59ded7e7-b8d8-4c47-ba1c-a2421bfbccc8","uuid":"e067e75c-e369-4192-a3ce-ec38c5454779","timestamp":"2026-08-31T06:14:31.837Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-11/kb-manifest.md","content":"`volumeMounts` mount that same claim wherever it lands.\n\n**Note what that does *not* say.** The storage does not move. Nothing is copied. **The claim\nis a namespaced API object with no node in it, and the volume behind it is cluster-scoped.**\nNeither cares which machine `web-1` is on today.\n\nFor the involuntary case the documentation is quotable directly: *\"If a Pod associated with a\nStatefulSet fails due to node failure, and the control plane creates a replacement Pod, the\nStatefulSet retains the existing PVC. The existing volume is unaffected, and the cluster will\nattach it to the node where the new Pod is about to launch.\"*\n\n**Identity is the mechanism.** A Deployment's replacement is a new Pod with a new name and no\nhistory. A StatefulSet's replacement for `web-1` must be *named* `web-1`, because the name is\nhow it finds `www-web-1`. Identity and storage are one mechanism seen from two sides — this\nis the payoff, and it is what Ch 6 could not say without §2–§4.\n\n⚠ **Wording provenance:** the 2026-08-25 capture flags the 08-24 wording of the reschedule\nsentences as differing load-bearingly (PersistentVolumes vs PersistentVolumeClaims). Ch 11\nstates the reschedule behavior **in the book's own words** and quotes only the verified 08-25\nnode-failure statement. Preserve that split; do not \"upgrade\" the paraphrase to a quotation.\n\n## ★ The claims outlive the workload\n\n> **A StatefulSet's PersistentVolumeClaims are not deleted when the Pod is deleted, or when\n> the StatefulSet is deleted. This must be done manually.**\n\nGoverned by `persistentVolumeClaimRetentionPolicy` — `whenDeleted` and `whenScaled`, each\ntaking `Delete` or `Retain`, **`Retain` the default for both**. *\"PVCs from the\nvolumeClaimTemplate are not affected when their Pod is deleted.\"*\n\nThe volumes behind them persist too: *\"the PersistentVolumes associated with the Pods'\nPersistentVolume Claims are not deleted when the Pods, or StatefulSet are deleted. This must\nbe done manually.\"* **Two objects, two survival facts, stated in different places** — the\nclaims are the ones found sitting in the namespace afterward.\n\nThe reason is a stated judgment call, not an oversight: *\"This is done to ensure data safety,\nwhich is generally more valuable than an automatic purge of all related StatefulSet\nresources.\"* Kubernetes chose the failure mode where forgotten volumes accumulate over the\none where a command destroys a database. Correctly — but the cleanup is yours.\n\n**Boundary:** these policies apply to **voluntary** removal only. A node dying is not a\nscale-down.\n\n**Deliberately omitted:** a sentence about a `StatefulSetAutoDeletePVC` feature gate whose\nstability stage could not be confirmed. The `Retain` default is the safe claim. Do not\nrestore the gate reference without a dated snapshot.\n\n## The opposite mechanism, held next to it\n\nA **generic ephemeral volume** also creates one PVC per Pod, and *\"when the Pod is deleted,\nthe Kubernetes garbage collector deletes the PVC.\"* **Exactly opposite deletion behavior.**\nThe difference is intent: an ephemeral volume is scratch space provisioned like real storage;\na StatefulSet's claim is real storage created by a controller. See [[volume-types-ephemeral]].\n\n## Constraints on later chapters\n\n- **Ch 16 §6** debugs this from the application side (B6 skeleton). Refers; does not redefine.\n- ⚑ **Ch 11 §6's pointer to Ch 9 is broken.** It cites `Ch 9 §6` for the headless Service and\n  per-Pod DNS names. Verified against shipped headings: **Ch 9 §5** is the headless Service,\n  **Ch 9 §6** is kube-proxy, **Ch 9 §7** is DNS names. Fix before ship; Ch 16 §6 should cite\n  §5 and §7, not §6.\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/pluggable-interfaces.md ===\n# Concept — The four pluggable interfaces\n\n**Collected at:** **Ch 17 §4 — \"Every Place Kubernetes Lets You In.\"** *Collects; does not\nredefine.*\n**Definition homes:** CRI → Ch 2 §4 · CNI → Ch 9 §1 · **CSI → Ch 11 §5** · CRDs → Ch 6 §8\n**Status:** ⚑ **two live naming/ordinal conflicts. Read both before drafting Ch 17 §4.**\n\n---\n\n## The set\n\n**CRI, CNI, CSI, CRDs.** B7 canonical form. Ch 11 §5 states it in exactly those four tokens,\nand Ch 11 is the chapter where the set closes.\n\n## The shape they share — what Ch 17 §4 asks the reader to state without help\n\n> **Each publishes a contract that lets someone outside the Kubernetes project supply an\n> implementation without editing core Kubernetes code.**\n\nCSI states it most explicitly — vendors can introduce new storage systems *\"without ever\nhaving to edit the core Kubernetes code\"* `[source: k8s-docs-volumes-2026-08-23]` — and the\nextension-points documentation groups CSI, CNI and CRI as infrastructure extensions alongside\nCRDs as API extensions `[source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23]`.\n\n⚠ **Grouping these four as *the* four pluggable interfaces is this book's framing, not a\nKubernetes ranking.** Ch 11 Practice Q12's answer key says so. Keep that disclosure.\n\nCh 11 Practice Q12 also fixes the boundaries of the claim: **A** (\"all four are DaemonSets\")\nis false of CRDs and of the CSI controller component; **B** (\"all four are cluster-scoped API\nobjects\") is false — CRI is not an API object at all; **D** (\"same release, versioned\ntogether\") is fabricated. Ch 17 §4 must not accidentally assert any of the three.\n\n## ⚑ Conflict 1 — Chapter 2 calls the fourth one \"API extensions\"\n\n- `chapter-02:598` and `chapter-02:930` (shipped): **\"CRI, CNI, CSI, and API extensions.\"**\n- B7 ledger, `chapter-10:1866`, and Ch 11 §5: **\"CRDs.\"**\n\nBoth surface forms are in shipped text. They are partially reconciled already — Ch 2's own\ninbound pointer at line 600 links its \"API extensions\" slot to `Ch 6 §8 — CRDs` — but\n**Ch 17 §4 is the section that meets both**, and reconciling them is its job. Chapter 11\nfollows the ledger and is correct; do not weaken it.\n\n## ⚑ Conflict 2 — Chapter 9 says CNI is the *second*, and it is the outlier\n\n- `chapter-02:914` (shipped): *\"This is the **first** of four times you will see this move.\n  Storage does it. Networking does it. The API itself does it.\"* → CRI, CSI, CNI, CRDs.\n- `chapter-09:1149` and `:1650` (shipped): CNI is *\"the **second** instance\"* / *\"**Second**\n  of the four pluggable interfaces.\"*\n- `chapter-10:1866` (shipped): the reader holds **three** — CRI (Ch 2), CRDs (Ch 6), CNI\n  (Ch 9).\n- Ch 11 §5: CSI is **\"the fourth\"** / **\"the last of the four.\"**\n\nBy encounter order the reader meets CRI at Ch 2 §4 and CRDs at Ch 6 §8 before CNI, making CNI\nthe **third**. Ch 2, Ch 10 and Ch 11 agree; **Ch 9 disagrees with all three.**\n**Recommendation: retrofit Ch 9 §8 prose and summary row, \"second\" → \"third.\"**\n\n## Resolved — a collision Ch 10 predicted and Ch 11 avoided\n\nThe B6 skeleton labels Ch 11 §5 *\"**Third** of the four pluggable interfaces\"*\n(`section-skeleton.md:157`). Ch 10's Stage 14 manifest flagged that this would land a\n\"third\" on a reader just told they held three. **Shipped Ch 11 ignored the skeleton and wrote\n\"the fourth\" / \"the last.\"** Correct.\n\n**Recommendation: drop the skeleton's ordinal annotations entirely.** They encode set-order,\nnot encounter-order, and encounter-order is what the reader experiences.\n\n## See also\n\n[[csi]] · [[absent-component-pattern]] — three of the four have a documented absent-component\nfailure mode, and Ch 11 §5 is where the reader is expected to stop asking *\"is the object\nthere?\"* and start asking *\"is the component there?\"*\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/absent-component-pattern.md ===\n# Concept — The absent-component pattern\n\n**Naming home:** Ch 3 §4 (where the name is coined)\n**Full treatment:** Ch 10 §3 and Ch 10 §8 (Zenith) · **two further instances at Ch 11 §3 and §5**\n**B3 cross-cutting theme:** #3 — retrieved *by name* at Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7\n**Status:** ⚑ **TWO CONFLICTS OPEN — canonical form, and instance count.**\n**⛔ DO NOT DRAFT Ch 13 §7 OR Ch 17 §7 UNTIL THE COUNT IS RESOLVED.**\n\n---\n\n## The rule\n\n> **An object without its component does nothing.**\n\nVerbatim from shipped Ch 3 §4; reproduced verbatim in Ch 10 §3, Ch 10 §8, and Ch 11's Voyage\nAhead. **Do not paraphrase this sentence in any chapter** — it is retrieved as a quotation.\n\nCh 10 §8's compression, which later chapters should reach for:\n\n> An object is a record of intent. Intent does not act.\n\n## ⚑ CONFLICT 1 — canonical form (carried from Ch 10's Stage 14)\n\n| Form | Where it lives | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **\"the absent-component pattern\"** | Shipped Ch 3 §4, reader-facing ×4 | **Recommended headword** |\n| \"an object without its component does nothing\" | Shipped Ch 3 §4; Ch 10 ×9; Ch 11 ×3 | The **rule sentence**. Not a name. |\n| \"The object exists; nothing happens without the component\" | B7 ledger only | **Zero occurrences in shipped text.** Do not adopt. |\n| \"Nothing Happens Without a Controller\" | Ch 10 §8 heading | A section title, not a term |\n\n**Recommendation:** headword = *the absent-component pattern* (naming home Ch 3 §4); rule\nsentence as above; instance ledger owned by Ch 10 §3.\n\n## ⚑ CONFLICT 2 — the instance count. **NEW at Ch 11. This is the blocking one.**\n\n**Two incompatible counting conventions are now in shipped and finalized text.**\n\n### Convention A — Chapter 10's, and it is graded\n\n`chapter-10:634` has the reader at three by Ch 10 §3, and `chapter-10:1567` — Practice Q18's\n**correct answer** — enumerates four by Ch 10 §7:\n\n1. `type: LoadBalancer` Service with no provider — **Ch 9 §3**\n2. Service whose selector matches no Pods — **Ch 9 §4**\n3. Ingress with no Ingress controller — **Ch 10 §3**\n4. NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement NetworkPolicy — **Ch 10 §7** (the only\n   silent one)\n\n`chapter-10:1795` marks option B — which names only Ch 10's two instances — wrong, with this\nrationale: *\"the common error here is **undercounting**, not misnaming.\"*\n\n### Convention B — Chapter 11's, which restarts at the Ingress\n\n- `draft-v2.md:587` — *\"This is the **third** sighting\"*: Ingress, NetworkPolicy, StorageClass\n- `draft-v2.md:889` — *\"three times in three sections: an Ingress without a controller, a\n  StorageClass without a provisioner, and now this. Here is **the fourth**\"*\n- `draft-v2.md:1402` — *\"the Ingress controller, the network plugin, the provisioner, and the\n  CSI driver — **four sightings**\"*\n\n**Chapter 11 drops both Chapter 9 instances** — the exact undercount Ch 10 grades as wrong.\nA reader who answered Ch 10 Q18 correctly is told six pages later that the StorageClass is\nthe third; by the count they were graded on, it is the fifth.\n\n**`draft-v2.md:889` is also internally inconsistent**: its own three-item list already\nincludes the current (CSI) case, and the sentence then announces \"the fourth.\" It silently\ndrops the NetworkPolicy instance that line 587 included two sections earlier. **The two\nenumerations inside Chapter 11 are not the same set.**\n\n### Why this blocks Ch 13 and Ch 17\n\nCh 10's Stage 14 shard reserved instance **#5 for Ch 13 §7** (`kubectl top` with no\nmetrics-server) and **#6 for Ch 17 §7** (VPA as an addon). Chapter 11 has inserted two\ninstances and renumbered from a different origin. **The conventions now diverge by exactly\ntwo**, and whichever ordinal Ch 13 uses contradicts a graded item in the other chapter.\n\n### Recommendation\n\n1. Amend Ch 11 §3/§5/Voyage Ahead to continue Convention A (StorageClass = fifth, CSI =\n   sixth). Faithful to graded canon; the ordinals get clumsy.\n2. **Preferred: stop numbering instances in prose.** Keep the rule and the enumeration, drop\n   the ordinal. Ch 10 already had to reconcile two counts in a dedicated paragraph\n   (`chapter-10:625`); Ch 11 is the third count, and the reconciliation cost now exceeds the\n   rhetorical benefit. **The list transfers; the number breaks.**\n\nEither way, `draft-v2.md:889`'s internal contradiction needs fixing regardless.\n\n**This shard deliberately does not pick a winner.** Both conventions are recorded above so\nno drafting stage can adopt one without seeing the other.\n\n## The instance ledger (Convention A ordering, origin Ch 9 §3)\n\n| # | Instance | Where | Announces itself? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `type: LoadBalancer` Service, no provider | Ch 9 §3 | Yes — `<pending>` |\n| 2 | Service whose selector matches no Pods | Ch 9 §4 | Yes — traffic fails |\n| 3 | Ingress with no Ingress controller | Ch 10 §3 | Yes — requests fail loudly |\n| 4 | NetworkPolicy on a plugin that does not implement it | Ch 10 §7 | **No — silent** |\n| 5 | **StorageClass naming a provisioner nobody deployed** | **Ch 11 §3** | Yes — claim unbound, Pod `Pending` |\n| 6 | **StorageClass naming a CSI driver never installed** | **Ch 11 §5** | Yes — same signature |\n| 7 | `kubectl top` with no metrics-server | Ch 13 §7 (planned) | Yes — command errors |\n| 8 | VPA, an addon not shipped by default | Ch 17 §7 (planned) | Yes |\n\n*Rows 5–6 are Ch 11's \"third\" and \"fourth\" under Convention B. Rows 7–8 were #5 and #6 in\nCh 10's shard and have shifted.*\n\n**Instances 5 and 6 are the same failure at two depths** — a StorageClass whose provisioner\nis a CSI driver nobody deployed *is* both — which is an argument for the un-numbered\ntreatment. Ch 11 §5's sources state it as a plain ordering requirement rather than a warning:\n*\"To use a CSI driver from a storage provider, you must **first** deploy it to your cluster,\"*\nand *\"the core of Kubernetes does not install that software for you.\"*\n\n## The asymmetry — provenance, keep the split\n\nInstances 1–3 and 5–8 announce themselves. **Instance 4 does not.** The plugin dependency and\nthe \"no effect\" consequence are **documented**\n`[source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24]`. The characterisation of that failure\nas *silent* is **the book's own inference**, labelled as one in Ch 10 §7. Do not promote it.\n\n## The transferable question\n\n> **What is watching this, and is it installed?**\n\nCh 10 §8 hands this to the reader; Ch 11 §5 restates it as *\"you stop asking 'is the object\nthere?' and start asking 'is the **component** there?'\"* Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7 should invoke\nit in these words.\n\n## Constraints on later chapters\n\n- Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7: retrieve **by name**; do not re-derive. **Blocked on Conflict 2.**\n- Any chapter adding an instance: extend the table, do not restate the rule.\n- **Never illustrate an instance as a broken or blocked object.** Every genuine instance is a\n  **correct** object with nothing wrong with it. Ch 10 Practice Q18 option D is built on that\n  distinction (a mismatched `pathType` is a manifest bug, not an instance), and Ch 11 §3's\n  framing depends on it too: *\"`kubectl get` shows it, `kubectl describe` shows a sensible\n  spec, and nothing happens.\"*\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n# Objective Coverage — KCNA\n\nObjective IDs (`D1.1`, `D2.1`, …) are a **Lodestar convention** from B1 domain analysis. CNCF\npublishes named competencies under each domain but does **not** number them and does **not**\npublish sub-competency weights. Domain-level weights are the only level the sources state.\n\n**Domain weights (2025-11-24 four-domain blueprint):** D1 Kubernetes Fundamentals 44% ·\n**D2 Container Orchestration 28%** · D3 Cloud Native Application Delivery 16% ·\nD4 Cloud Native Architecture 12%.\n\n> **BACKFILL REQUIRED.** Stage 14 did not run for Chapters 1–9. Rows below cover Chapters 10\n> and 11, plus the D2.1 first-coverage fact verified against shipped Ch 9. Objectives D1.x,\n> D2.3, D3.x and D4.x are unlogged. Source of truth for the backfill:\n> `.pipeline-state/book-outline/chapter-lineup.md` and `domain-analysis.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Objective-level\n\n| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| D2.1 — Networking | Chapter 9 | deep — completed Chapter 10 | — |\n| D2.2 — Security | Chapter 10 (boundary only) | partial — NetworkPolicy only; full coverage lands Chapter 12 | — |\n| **D2.4 — Storage** | **Chapter 11** | **deep — sole coverage; no other chapter is assigned D2.4** | — |\n\n**D2.2 note.** B1 sequencing implication #6: NetworkPolicy sits in both D2.1 and D2.2; teach\nit once, in Networking, and cross-bear from Security. Chapter 10 is the sole definition home;\nChapter 12 §9 refers and must not duplicate.\n\n**D2.4 note.** B2 sequencing constraint: *\"Storage precedes StatefulSet's full treatment —\nCh 6 introduces, Ch 11 completes, cross-beared both ways.\"* Honored. Ch 6 §6's five deferred\nverbs (provisioned / requested / sized / reclaimed / shared) are all closed at Ch 11 §4's\nreconciliation table; Ch 6 retains **identity**, which Ch 11 §6 joins rather than restates.\n\n## Concept-level — D2.4, all 20 B1 concepts\n\nWalked row by row against `domain-analysis.md:232–255`. **20 of 20 covered. No gaps.**\n\n| B1 concept | Covered in | Depth |\n|---|---|---|\n| PersistentVolume (PV) | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n| PV lifecycle independence | Ch 11 §1, §2 | deep |\n| PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n| Pods:PVCs :: node resources:Pods | Ch 11 §2 | deep — quoted, not paraphrased |\n| StorageClass | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n| Static provisioning | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n| Dynamic provisioning | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n| Disabling dynamic provisioning (`\"\"`) | Ch 11 §3 | deep |\n| Binding | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n| Unbound claims | Ch 11 §2 | deep |\n| Using (Pod references a PVC) | Ch 11 §2 | deep — the section's Fixed Point |\n| Reclaiming | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| Retain | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| Delete | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| Recycle | Ch 11 §4 | adequate — named, deprecation stated |\n| ReadWriteOnce (RWO) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| ReadOnlyMany (ROX) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| ReadWriteMany (RWX) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP) | Ch 11 §4 | deep |\n| StatefulSet + PV | Ch 11 §6 | deep |\n\n## Trap coverage — D2.4\n\n**7 of 7 (#63–#69) addressed**, verified line by line against `domain-analysis.md:571–577`.\nThe Exam Alert reproduces the complete D2 storage block in order with faithful corrections.\n\n| Trap | Where corrected |\n|---|---|\n| #63 \"A PVC binds to any PV that's big enough\" | §2 + Bearings #1 Q4 + Practice Q13 |\n| #64 Reversing PV and PVC | §2 Fixed Point + Bearings #1 Q3 + Practice Q2 |\n| #65 \"ReadWriteOnce means one Pod\" | §4 Fixed Point + Bearings #2 Q1 |\n| #66 \"Deleting a PVC always keeps the data\" | §4 + Bearings #2 Q2 + Practice Q3 |\n| #67 \"Retain means immediately reusable\" | §2, §4 + Bearings #2 Q4 + Practice Q15 |\n| #68 Expecting `Recycle` to be live | §4 |\n| #69 Class `\"\"` uses the default | §3 + Bearings #2 Q5 |\n\nThe chapter's own arithmetic about trap distribution checks out: §4's *\"five of the seven…\nfour of them are here\"* (#69 in §3, #65–#68 in §4) and Bearings #2's *\"four of the exam\ntraps… in this checkpoint alone\"* (Q1→#65, Q2→#66, Q4→#67, Q5→#69).\n\n## Research gaps closed\n\n| Gap | Description | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **G11** | *CNI, CSI, and CRI as the pluggable interfaces — \"CSI is entirely absent.\"* | **Closed by Ch 11 §5.** Sourced to the CSI specification's own objective statement, plus the driver's two-component deployment shape. |\n| **G12** | *Volume types other than PV/PVC: emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret volumes, projected, ephemeral.* | **Closed by Ch 11 §1**, which covers all five plus `downwardAPI`, `subPath`, `nfs`, and `local`. |\n| **G25** | Gateway API detail | Closed by Ch 10 §5. |\n\n## Research gaps still open that touch Chapter 11\n\n- **PV phase count.** The concept page enumerates four, the v1 API reference five. A further\n  retrieval of `/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/#phase` would settle it and clear\n  the `Released`/`Failed` prose for direct quotation. See `concepts/pv-phases.md`.\n- **CSI driver implementations.** No cached snapshot enumerates them; the shipped chapter\n  genericized its three named drivers accordingly. Target: the `kubernetes-csi` drivers list.\n- **`allowVolumeExpansion` semantics.** The grow-not-shrink sentence is not attested in any\n  cached snapshot and was **not** written into the chapter. Do not add it without one.\n- **No exam-logistics source is cached.** Unchanged from Ch 10. Chapter 11 complies with the\n  standing constraint — its header discloses that *\"CNCF publishes four domain weights and no\n  sub-competency weights; the D2.4 identifier is this book's own numbering, not CNCF's.\"*\n\n---\n\n*Stage 14 · Chapters 10–11 · 2026-08-25.*\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n# Retrieval-Practice Ledger — KCNA\n\nWhich earlier-chapter topics have been retrieval-tested, and where. Targets are set by B3\nretrieval architecture: Ch 3 at 10%, Ch 4 at 15%, then **20–25% through Ch 18**, with a\n**spacing floor from Ch 8 on — at least one item must come from ≥4 chapters back.**\n\nSoundings are **excluded from the budget** by design: skill Part 11 requires them to be\nanswerable from prerequisites, which in this book means earlier chapters, so every Soundings\nblock is already a spaced retrieval event. Counting them would distort their calibration\npurpose.\n\n> ⚠ **`.pipeline-state/book-outline/retrieval-architecture.md` on disk contains a\n> blocked-write error message, not the B3 artifact.** The substantive design survives as\n> prose inside that file and the targets above are taken from it, but the file should be\n> regenerated.\n\n> **BACKFILL REQUIRED.** Stage 14 did not run for Chapters 1–9. Their `[retrieval: chN]` tags\n> are in the shipped files and unlogged here. Chapters 10 and 11 are complete.\n\n---\n\n## Backward — earlier material tested in a later chapter\n\n| Tested topic | Original chapter | Retested in |\n|---|---|---|\n| Service-type ladder; HTTP vs non-HTTP exposure | ch 9 §3 | ch 10 — Bearings #1 Q1; Practice Q2 |\n| The absent-component rule, stated back | ch 3 §4 | ch 10 — Bearings #1 Q5; Practice Q18 |\n| Control loop / the controller pattern | ch 3 §6 | ch 10 — Practice Q7 |\n| Labels and selectors as the universal join | ch 4 §5 | ch 10 — Bearings #3 Q1; Practice Q16 |\n| The writable container layer; what a restart discards | ch 2 §2 | **ch 11 — Bearings #1 Q2** |\n| The four pluggable interfaces, as a set | ch 2 §4/§5 | **ch 11 — Practice Q12** |\n| ServiceAccount token via a projected volume | ch 5 §6 | **ch 11 — Practice Q1** |\n| StatefulSet ordinal identity | ch 6 §6 | **ch 11 — Bearings #3 Q3** ⚠️ thin |\n| Filter phase; the unschedulable Pod | ch 7 §2 | **ch 11 — Bearings #2 Q3** |\n| The absent-component pattern | ch 10 §3 | **ch 11 — Practice Q8** |\n\n## Chapter 11 compliance\n\n| Check | Target | Actual | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Retrieval share of graded items | 20–25% (B3); outline target 20% | **7 of 32 = 21.9%** (15 Bearings + 17 Practice) | ✅ |\n| Spacing floor (≥4 chapters back) | ≥1 item | ch 2 is **nine** back, ×2 items | ✅ |\n| Tagged items land on covered material | 7 of 7 | **6 of 7** — see ⚑ below | ❌ |\n| Question inventory | 8 Soundings, ≥10 Bearings across ≥2 checkpoints | 8 + 15 (3 × 5) + 17 = **40** | ✅ |\n\n**Soundings note.** Ch 11's block is unusually load-bearing: **six of its eight questions are\nretrieval** from Ch 2 §2/§4, Ch 4 §3, Ch 5 §1/§6, Ch 6 §6, Ch 6 §8, Ch 9 §1 and Ch 10's\nclosing. Excluded from the budget per B3, but worth recording — the chapter gets substantially\nmore spaced retrieval than its 21.9% suggests.\n\n## ⚑ One failed retrieval anchor — Chapter 11 Practice Q4\n\nQ4 asks the reader to identify \"PersistentVolume cluster-scoped; PersistentVolumeClaim\nnamespaced\" and tags it **`[retrieval: ch4]`**.\n\n**`grep -c \"PersistentVolumeClaim\" chapter-04-records-of-intent.md` → 0.** Chapter 4 §3 names\nNodes, PersistentVolumes and StorageClasses as its cluster-scoped examples; it never mentions\nPVCs. The B7 ledger corroborates independently — it records PVC's first appearance as **Ch 6\n§6**, and ⚑6 lists only \"PersistentVolume and StorageClass\" as appearing early in Ch 4.\n\nThe **fact** is correct and Ch 11 §2 sources it properly. The **retrieval tag** is not: the\nreader is asked to retrieve something never deposited. The same root cause makes Ch 11 §2's\nprose claim (*\"Chapter 4 taught you… You now have the reason rather than the rule\"*)\nover-credit Chapter 4.\n\n**Fixes, in preference order:**\n1. **Rewrite Q4 around Chapter 4's own cluster-scoped trio** (Node, PersistentVolume,\n   StorageClass) — a genuine Ch 4 anchor, already graded at `chapter-04:733` and `:1308`,\n   which holds the rate at 21.9%.\n2. Retag as untagged and let it stand as a current-chapter §2 item — **drops the rate to\n   6 of 32 = 18.8%, below band.**\n3. Leave it. Not recommended; it overstates the book's retrieval coverage.\n\n## Forward obligations Chapter 11 creates\n\n| Topic Ch 11 owns | Must be retrieved in | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| `secret` volumes on tmpfs | **Ch 12 §4** | Ch 11 hands over **half an argument by name** — file mounts vs environment variables. Ch 12 §4 owes the other half. |\n| `hostPath` as the workload-to-host hole | **Ch 12 §5** | Named and deferred explicitly; also owns the `ReadOnlyMany`-is-not-a-permission-system pointer. **Ch 12 must not re-derive the risk.** |\n| An unbound claim as a `Pending` Pod | **Ch 13 §2** | Ch 11 supplies the mechanism, Ch 13 the diagnosis. |\n| Per-replica PVC; ordinal identity + storage | **Ch 16 §6** | B6 skeleton line 243. Refers; does not redefine. ⚑ cite Ch 9 §5 and §7, **not** §6. |\n| CSI as the fourth interface | **Ch 17 §4** | *Collects; does not redefine.* ⚑ two naming conflicts — read `concepts/pluggable-interfaces.md` first. |\n| The absent-component pattern | **Ch 13 §7, Ch 17 §7** | ⛔ **BLOCKED** on the instance-count conflict. |\n\n## Open gaps\n\n**1. ⚑ NEW — the absent-component instance count is contested.** Chapter 11 restarts the\ncount at the Ingress and drops Chapter 9's two instances — the exact undercount Ch 10 Practice\nQ18 grades as wrong (*\"the common error here is undercounting\"*). Chapter 11's own two\nenumerations (`draft-v2.md:587` and `:889`) are not the same set, and `:889` calls the third\nitem in its own three-item list \"the fourth.\" **Blocks Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7.** Full detail\nin `concepts/absent-component-pattern.md`.\n\n**2. CSI driver architecture is taught but never assessed.** The controller Deployment plus\nper-node DaemonSet — promised by name at `chapter-02:600` — appears in **zero of Ch 11's 40\nquestions**. The chapter's own AUTHOR-REVIEW records why: the curriculum rebalance claimed\nBearings #3's fifth slot for a `volumeClaimTemplates` item, and the two findings competed for\none seat. **The Practice set is the right home if a slot is found** — restoring it to Bearings\n#3 would push CSI to 3 of 5 there against 1 of 17 in Practice, which is the imbalance the\nrebalance was correcting.\n\n**3. north-south / east-west is taught but never assessed.** Carried from Chapter 10, where it\nappears in zero of 41 questions despite a 🪢 Mnemonic and a Chapter Summary row. **Still\nopen**; Chapter 11 does not touch it.\n\n**4. ⚑ Chapter 9 undercounts the pluggable interfaces.** Ch 9 §8 calls CNI *\"the second\ninstance\"*; Ch 2 line 914, Ch 10, and Ch 11 all make it the third. **Still open.**\nRecommended retrofit: Ch 9 §8 prose and summary row, \"second\" → \"third.\"\n\n## Resolved since Chapter 10\n\n**Ordinal collision at Ch 11 §5 — RESOLVED.** Ch 10's log predicted that the B6 skeleton's\n*\"Third of the four pluggable interfaces\"* label (`section-skeleton.md:157`) would land a\n\"third\" on a reader just told they held three. **Shipped Ch 11 §5 ignored the skeleton and\nwrote \"The fourth is CSI\" / \"the last of the four.\"** Consistent with Ch 2, Ch 10, and the B7\ncanonical set. **Recommendation: drop the skeleton's ordinal annotations** — they encode\nset-order, not encounter-order.\n\n---\n\n*Stage 14 · Chapters 10–11 · 2026-08-25.*\n=== END WRITE ===\n```\n\n---\n\n## Recommended fixes carried forward from this stage\n\n**Blocking — resolve before Ch 13 §7 drafts.**\n\n1. **The absent-component instance count** (⚑A). Chapter 11 undercounts against a Chapter 10 graded item that names undercounting as the error, and its own two enumerations disagree. My recommendation is to stop numbering instances in prose and keep the list.\n\n**Fix before ship — chapter-text edits, all small.**\n\n2. **§6's `Ch 9 §6` pointer** → `Ch 9 §7`, and attribute the headless Service to Ch 9 §5.\n3. **§2's Ch 4 attribution** → claim only the PV half; present the PVC half as new. Rewrite **Practice Q4** around Ch 4's own cluster-scoped trio.\n4. **Expand NFS and LUN on first use** and register NFS, LUN, iSCSI, EBS. NFS carries two graded items and is nowhere in the book in full.\n5. **`draft-v2.md:889`'s internal contradiction** — a three-item list that includes the current case, followed by \"here is the fourth.\"\n\n**Author's call — low severity.**\n\n6. §5's stale AUTHOR-REVIEW cites `ebs.csi.aws.com` \"used in this chapter's Taking Your Bearings #3\"; that string now appears only inside the comment.\n7. Soundings scoring note says six of eight questions are two-part; seven are.\n8. §1 says Ch 4 \"hedged\" the ConfigMap update rule; `chapter-04:762` states it flatly and sourced. One word.\n9. `finalizer` and `ENOSPC` need either a one-clause gloss or a B7 ambient-tier assignment.\n\n**Infrastructure.**\n\n10. **`retrieval-architecture.md` on disk is a blocked-write error message**, not the B3 artifact — unchanged since Chapter 10 flagged it. Regenerate.\n11. **The B6 skeleton's ordinal annotations should be dropped.** Chapter 11 correctly ignored one; the next chapter might not.\n\n## What I'd act on first\n\nThe instance count, ahead of the integration report's two findings. Both of those are one-line edits with obvious fixes; this one has no obvious fix, a nearer deadline, and no natural discovery point. Ch 13 §7 and Ch 17 §7 are contractually required to retrieve the pattern by name and by count, and the two live conventions now differ by exactly two. Whichever a drafter picks, it contradicts a graded answer key in the other chapter — and nothing downstream will flag it, because both counts are internally coherent and both cite real instances.","numLines":537,"startLine":1466,"totalLines":2002}}}
